{"body":{"text":["Well, some of my first thoughts are that's a dimension, maybe crucial. Sure, it doesn't touch the contents of the \\[holons\\], which is great. You probably don't want that always, but it might still be very necessary and important information. However, if you own that dimension, then you do. The only question is if you break out a space with things that is on a dimension and give it to someone else and it doesn't contain that dimension, then of course a certain amount of information is lost per se. Secondly, we may be able to use the rule knowledge and briefs as an example of things coming and living in multiple stores or being dimensions, as an example, and the knowledge structure is something that definitely should be able to be partitioned, because it's definitely \\[holons\\] sometimes that do not care too much about what is above. Right, that's definitely the case. And that if from a peer-to-peer point of view, there's definite solutions where you together virtualize a knowledge space, but maybe you own different parts of it. So that's a good example to orient ourselves around. And so we know there that something currently has to be owned, and that's what briefs already today are. It's placed in a place, and it's then its ownership. But you can use quote block cards, right, in Markdown. So that's where you give something the proper presentation, but it's still owned somewhere else. And that's important for briefs, and in a way, the fact that it had to have ownership in the briefs, I found a little bit of a problem. And so that still makes me a little bit sad. And now that we are settling that things have to have ownership, it does leave me a bit sad as well, because ultimately it's only when you break something away that the ownership perhaps matters from a store point of view. And then if it's placed in both, then it will be included if you take one chunk where it's linked, or if you take the other, it doesn't quite matter. So from a peer point of view, that's that makes me sad to have to force something to be in one place. But the thing about dimensions there as well, dimensions in briefs is the fact that multiple places can mount something, I think, right? So if it's I have a tree over the workflows, of all of the people using an application, let's say, and it goes from the top level, which talks about the workflows divided into 5 pieces, then each workflow is divided into a few pieces, and then each of those workflows are divided into details, and then each of those are defined or divided into the very action points within the application when you use them. So they were breaking things down. Then maybe what we're doing is that we're separately talking about audience, or we're talking about a specific group of people or another specific group of people. Now, those people might have only some workflows pertain to them. That's a dimension. Right. That means that the content is actually part of a workflow, but then it's also part of an audience \\[holon\\]. Which has 2 \\[subholons\\]. That now are actual types. So then you place them in there, as well, as citizens, not as links. And then when you are on that workflow, you can see that, hey, I'm a part of the workflow, but hey, I'm also part of that. Now, that kind of thinking is has been done in a world where I've only been thinking about briefs. And I've been thinking, as I look upon that knowledge, that, hey, you know, maybe workflows is to be something different. You know, maybe this is kind of more of the typed ontology. ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M6YBY3G00007RQ"}," We have workflows, we have goals, we have domain knowledge, and these things have an ontological relationship in terms of the tool making under the rule repository. So please read the rule if you haven't. So these are some of the angles that I think about on this topic."]},"id":"1M6YBY3G000073H","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
