{"body":{"text":["No, this step is not a fragment. I'm sorry, it's not. It's whole in itself. And if I were to take the audience and pull it away and take it with me, then each whole workflow therein would be whole, and the audience would be whole, and whole workflows. Would it still have the same big tree? No. Is there still a value in the same big tree? Yes. But is it whole as it is? Yes. So I was merely here comparing that in this case, it might actually be so that the workflow tree is more important. As if these briefs were not briefs, but they were \\[holons\\]. Like, we actually made workflow into an actual type, right? And used that. Like, if we actually used the tool making primitives of the rule and built it into more structured data, not just briefs, then yeah, it would make sense that it's a workflow. It's not as part of an audience. But that's not a point for you to take all the way. It is an example, right? I was just being honest with the example. But in terms of the wholeness, they're definitely whole. I think that's part of what we're doing with the practice of the stories. If I take something and bring it with me, what's that mean? Now, the same thing goes with favorites. If I have favorites, then I build favorites. I'm responsible for favorites. If I want to take favorites with me and go and leave, of course, I should be able to do that. Now, that might come with problems. I might have functions in my favorites, and those functions may be instance of the type function. And so, of course, I can't take it completely, but that's another question. And that's something that's going to happen anyways, I think, right? So unless we want to go completely strict and say that you can't have instances of other things in your own things, but that doesn't make any sense. That's definitely the case. You rely upon other foundations to build yours. That's completely fine. At least if you take something and take it with you, and then there are the kind of outside things that you still need to bring with you. Let's say that you do bring them with you. They still have their ID. They still have their version. So the day that you get in touch with their, and you know, maybe actually connect the root store where those came from, they would plug back in right into that. Right. Now, the question of dimensions, and something that I've been thinking about, because we are also on the topic of briefs. Now, briefs are really quite stupid in a way. The alternative is the typed wholeness of \\[OpenLight\\], which is completely, you know, data completely. But our briefs are the opposite. Now, I've been thinking that maybe what we do is that some briefs, because before we didn't have files, this is very interesting. Now that we get into here, we kind of don't have files anymore. We have an important breaking point when we are linking a reference in the prose, then it is a breaking point. But if we're defining it as a part, then it is a part of a file, right? But now when we're in substrate, we're not constrained to files anymore. Files are still a reading experience with subheadings. So it still makes sense that we still confine it to, you know, just a set of few headings, because otherwise you can't show it as a proper document. So it's still, there's still some bad logic in that. But if we think upon it without the division of the files, not every file has its own ontological meaning. And what I've been thinking when I've been working with the surface and with the pilot is that I know that one of the coming things I want to do is that in the \\[holon\\] for workflow, in the \\[holon\\] for domain, etc., I want to say that this is a dimension. All briefs under this is part of this dimension. That means I want to give it maybe an icon, a graphic. I want to give it a color. Maybe I want to give it more, even more visual characteristics. I've been thinking about, you know, maybe this dimension actually gets its address, gets its thing. So whene