{"body":{"text":["Honour the primitives. Functional programming makes the function first-class: something to pass, return and compose directly. Unix gives programs a shared way to cooperate through explicit inputs and outputs, without reaching into one another's internals. Both let useful capabilities support combinations they did not have to anticipate."]},"id":"1M75B2JFT3Z10QC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A function should give one coherent purpose at its level. Its name expresses that purpose, not an inventory of its parts. Extract a function when it gives the caller a useful understanding without requiring the mechanics beneath it. This is the rule's gradient in code."]},"id":"1M75B2JFT3Z10S1","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Favour pure functions: explicit inputs determine the result without mutating those inputs or external state. Separate that reasoning from effects, whose execution requires care about timing, failure and changes to the world. Keep failure distinguishable from a valid result; recovery must not hide what went wrong."]},"id":"1M75B2JFT3Z10TT","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
