{"body":{"text":["3. Integrity and flow"]},"id":"1M75B2JFT3Z10VN","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Give each fact an authoritative home. Reference it rather than maintain competing copies, and derive what follows from it rather than store another value to keep in agreement. Explicit identities and relationships keep information coherent as it changes."]},"id":"1M75B2JFT3Z10X1","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Let data do work that would otherwise require logic. A better representation can remove conditionals and special cases. Normalise shared facts where that clarifies their relationships; the aim is integrity, not flatness for its own sake."]},"id":"1M75B2JFT3Z10YE","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Describe transformations rather than coordinate mutations. Familiar operations such as mapping and filtering expose intent without a private vocabulary. Keep the flow traceable from source through derivation to consumer, so understanding a result does not require untangling changes in both directions."]},"id":"1M75B2JFT3Z1101","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
