{"body":{"text":["The one engine stands at its smallest step: `site/host.ts` serves the site, each store live, and one engine over them, every store attached only to be read and taken again when its ref moves, the home a folder of the host's own; a page it serves is told the engine is there, and mounts to it over a socket that answers only the host's own origin and attaches nothing. Written beside it at the command line, a commit reaches the page without anything polled. A page published elsewhere still starts an engine of its own."]},"id":"1M75TESNY3NP032","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The one engine closed, green, 245 tests, and seen live: a commit made at the command line drawn on an open page with nothing polled, the page saying when its engine is lost and coming back whole with it. Read after three ways: against How the code is written, and for faults by Fable and Opus side by side, as the author asked. Each found one grave fault the other missed, Fable's not drastically more significant, so Fable is set aside for now and Opus reads in its place. Mended from the three: one host to a home, a store never written hosted and heard, renewals in turn and tried again, the page reloading only after an engine that had answered, its addresses kept as paths, what was never answered answered when the socket goes, what is no message refused in words. Left, in the thread: the tools through the engine, a refs folder replaced on Linux, renewals reading every block again, stores served to any origin."]},"id":"1M75TWX2JQQ405G","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
