{"body":{"text":["An index carried from commit to commit closed, green, 286 tests: each store's index kept beside the very holons it was made of, a store a commit did not touch keeping it, a read of history sharing it; 549 ms to 12 for the index after a commit to the home, 555 to none for a history read. Attacked by Opus before, which made it carrying whole stores before patching one; read by Opus after, which found a field holding every field before it in memory, a fault older than the cycle, now mended, and tests proving identity where answers were meant, now held against a fresh index. Left: a changed store's index patched by what changed, and the holders each field still builds whole."]},"id":"1M76364WM8Q906Z","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The index is kept from field to field, on a shelf each field hands to the next: each store's beside the holons it was made of, each by the block its version was read from, and the types they are read through. A store a commit did not touch keeps its index, and one read anew for history shares it. A store a commit changed has its index patched: the holons whose version changed, came or went, whose type changed, or which another store came to claim first, are said again, and nothing else; every list they are not in is shared with the index before, and the patched index answers exactly as one made fresh, in the same order. That asks that the store give its holons in order by id, as glass's does; one that does not, as git's, is made fresh, and so is one changed in a quarter of its holons or more. A store opened again after a commit keeps the blocks already read, as each is named by its hash. In main, 116 thousand holons and 690 thousand facts, a commit of one holon reads the store again in about 90 milliseconds where it took 500, and patches the index in about 65 where it took 420. Not yet: what holds each holon, made again for every field in about 130."]},"id":"1M764J2HT6WZ07A","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
