{"body":{"text":["So let it be said that this experiment today of using this map this amount of invariant this kind of running ahead, as we might call it, something that hasn't felt right. We've grown in complexity, we've added effort, with the type of governance left to you, and I don't trust it. I don't trust the cycles and how they just so easily settle into the mechanics and way of doing things and the attacks and everything. What I prompted, I think, is just something that the LLMs and the harness just couldn't embody fully. Now, there is lots of valuable knowledge uncovered, lots of interesting decisions, then when it comes to the technicalities, the code and everything, I have a feeling that maybe 50% of what I expected to be done will be held was not done, and I think there's lots of obvious things when I look at the code that just gives me the feeling that what I've said long ago in all of this and many important things just simply are not respected and honored. So we're ending this method here and from here you will introspect to the sessions and to what was said, and build a list. It's not a list, it's ontology, it's understanding. Right, it's structure, it's simple to understand and read, it's not a list of 20 things naturally, it is the primary things that may be broken down into sub things if need be, that aren't right, that's what I said I wanted the plan. I wanted the knowledge. Whatever we open up and understand on our goals to be ontologically structured in the field. Because that is simple. That is easy to hold. It isn't much data to parse. It's something to build around. So no, we're going to have to stop. We're going to have to absorb. Reflect, and I'm going to have to get in here as well, most likely, and critique and spend effort on recuperating from this running ahead."]},"id":"1M767EDPK1WV0AZ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The method stopped, nothing more written. Read from the author's own words across the sessions and held against the code, four things are not right, the first holding the rest. The understanding is not in the field; the mechanics are: long prose, self-marks and threads written by sessions about themselves, while the author asked three times for a view to witness the work. Primitives the author named are not honoured: a holon given to the first store that claims it, where they said there is nothing first; stores still named in a file, not declared in the field; the engine still needing a store; kinds as strings; the reader's root. The code does not follow the direction given for it: one fact worked out in several places, machinery added. And the way of working ran ahead, as said on 2026-11-07 and 2026-11-10 too: a question made into two cycles, things asked many times before they were done, review that checked the work against itself and found none of this."]},"id":"1M767EE5Y66T05F","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Good. That does highlight some of the things I see as well. Just immediately looking up on the code, we several things I've said are not aligned. But then code is also unrealistic. Something like the kernel is 1,000 lines long, there's just a depth now. It gives me the ick a little bit. So we're gonna have to work with it. Yes, as I look upon the file system, there's all of these tools in Author that I didn't think were supposed to be there. So I look upon components, I wonder if there are some unnecessary things made. I also see all kinds of JSONs. Yet the point was for knowledge to be in dot directories. Not only knowledge, the field, as the expressions as files from the expressions really are to be in the field as well, aren't they not? So what's the point of that? The engine is a lot more than I've read. But makes me question if the internals are really understood properly. Integration's also hold lots of JSONs and things which I don't understand. And there's all kinds of creation of holons inside of TypeScript files all over the place as well. But we have a good piece of things to work with as a start. It's good to see that you have the capability of cutting through the mess. That's important to know. It shows that maybe an appropriate workflow is not impossible. Just the way that we orchestrate the things just didn't pan"]},"id":"1M767EEN0NKG08J","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["What the author named is in the tree: seventeen JSON files and eleven. expr functions in components/, compiled into the store by components/make.ts, so every type, function and mount has two homes and the file is the one edited; nine files making holons in TypeScript; the author tools writing knowledge past the engine; files of 400 to 850 lines, about 11 thousand lines of code to 4 thousand of tests. The cutting through came when the mechanics stopped and the author's words were read directly, so a way of working with their words as the measure seems possible."]},"id":"1M767EF4MPDT034","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I don't care too much about orchestrating workflow now. I have no need to run ahead. I saw potential and that potential wasn't realized yet, and that's fine. But for those 11 lines of code, it is strong reasoning. It is grounding in the kinds of principles I've collected a bit of in the substrate, grounding in, of course, these are the things that I say over and over again, because still we don't have a foundation where I'm living and breathing the field yet. When I do, of course, my attention on that space and keeping it tidy and nice will be there. But really, you know, functional programming to me is understanding its ontology. It's understanding the nature of programming. And not misunderstanding the nature and working against it. And so with that, that's a certain piece of the spectrum. Another piece of the spectrum is understanding what we're building, our primitives themselves. And everything there between. And doing it in a style that is breathable, simple, that names things according to this. It's a whole nuance of things that you can embody. But it's exactly what's been practiced in the rule. We have the ability, once we become ontological enough about knowledge and about prose, to look upon what we want to convey and orchestrate it appropriately. And so the same thing can be done with code if you have that sufficient ability to decompose what it is you want to do and piece that together. So there is both that technical depth that may be solved, but then there is all of the things that I have expressed that were never made, and it's kind of big things as well."]},"id":"1M767EFKYR5Y09E","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Two works, and they need different work. How the code is written is the rule applied to code: know the nature of programming, know what is built, then decompose what the code is to say and piece it together, naming each part by what it is; the kernel is depth without structure because it was written outward from mechanics. And what was expressed and never made is bigger, the primitives themselves, so it comes before, or beside, the code being written well: careful work on code built around a first claim would be careful work on the wrong ground."]},"id":"1M767EG4Z6KB036","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
