{"body":{"text":["I am considering that's another approach, is rewriting the wiki completely. And that is, taking it to the highest levels first. Because really, how complicated is everything we've set at heart? Everything is simple. And if we work under the gradient, then maybe that's the right thing to do. But that's kind of the spec-based approach. That might not be necessary. To get this kind of improvement. Could be done later as well, just saying that I consider it, because ultimately what you're doing with an outline here is very close to what one would do if one were to write the higher layers of the knowledge topology. Fresh from scratch. Without being biased. Of how the previous wiki is written."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZ0Z7","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session agreed that the design and the top of a fresh wiki are the same act: before code, it has to say what glass is, its primitives and how they stand on each other, at the highest level and simplest terms, under the gradient. So it would write the design in that form, highest levels first, fresh, without reading the old wiki's wording, from the author's directives, the primitives as the author said them, and the code as it is, each level broken down only as far as the rewrite needs. If it reads well it can become the wiki's new top, the old wiki kept beneath as history; if not, it is still the plan. It would not do the full spec-first rewrite of the wiki now, the extra cost the author named, since the code does not need it to improve."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZ13X","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
