{"body":{"text":["The session took the answers as settling six of the seven decisions and folded them into the plan, still only in the scratchpad. The engine keeps no store; it mounts stores so work can be done, read, write and run, and persisting an attachment set in a field is possible but not a priority. The site says the stores it attaches in its own `.ts`, so `site/make.ts` and `site.json` go; a git browser might run its integration on demand as a repository is opened. The author tools go; writing is done through the engine's command line, so the plan's first step gives that command line the ability to commit; practices and principles can live as general skills, not code. Components become holons in `.glass` linking to their code; the `declared` export in each `.tsx` goes and making a component builds its code and writes nothing else; in this rewrite they get common sense, simple composing components stay, custom glue goes, the interface proper comes later. Loading code from the field waits until the rest is in place and integrations are decoupled; for now components and integrations are the only code; whether git leads toward the engine running programs, as in OpenLight, is marked unexplored. The root store mounting everything flat is left to common sense and later refactors. The order is now integrations/glass, language, engine, site, then interface and components, kept light."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZ29F","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
