{"body":{"text":["The session set out what each question turns on. On 1: it matters only when copies differ, which happens when a whole has been taken into its own store and changed there, or changed in one store while the other was not attached; the choice is which version is seen and whether the difference shows. \"The first attached\" is arbitrary and silent; common sense would be that the version from the later act wins and the field marks the holon as diverged, never silently hidden. On 2, common sense as the author said: if every store the act touched is attached and none has moved since, the engine finishes it by moving the remaining refs; otherwise writing to that store is refused with a message naming the act and the stores missing, until they are attached."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZ443","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["On 3: today there are two separate engines; `bun run serve` runs one for the page, and every command-line call starts a fresh one that loads the stores each time, about a second per call. The question is whether the command line should talk to the server's engine when one runs: faster, one field kept warm, no two writers on one store. The catch is that the server today only reads the repository's stores and writes just the home, so it would become the writer of the repository's stores."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZ46Y","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["On 4, settled: a store's root says which types it admits, a commit bringing any other is refused; git's store admits only git's types. On 5, a trade: `holon`, `whole`, `in` and `out` answer by identity, while patterns answer across the field but need the store's index, which needs the whole store loaded; keeping the four built in keeps reading one holon cheap; common sense says keep them and revisit if patterns become cheap without the whole store. On 6, memory for now, noted. It asked for answers on 1 and 3 before writing all six into the outline in one commit."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZ4AB","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
