{"body":{"text":["The synchronous-evaluator question is grounded too: the design says a pausing evaluator is to be measured before it is chosen, not designed in now. Today's evaluator is short (159 lines) and synchronous, works over every store read whole, and throws and starts again when it needs a store's history; reading a holon or a whole by id exists twice, in `field.ts` and among the natives. A pausing evaluator could merge those and make reads lazy one holon at a time, at the cost that every step pauses and resumes and every native reading the field must be written for that. It will measure both on the tests and the bench before saying which is better."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZ9YH","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
