{"body":{"text":["The field was corrected as the session had said and committed (6dd6cd5). Next it measured where a read spends its time today; the numbers already said a lot. It looked at how the natives call back into the evaluator and counted what a full move would touch: both functions only walk downward, through `get` and `parts`, so they are the reads laziness can serve. The cold measures were still running, one case after another, printing only at the end."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZA1A","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["What about thread safety etc?"]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZA1J","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A paused read is safe in one way and not yet in another; the cold measures were still running."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZA28","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["No races on memory: JavaScript runs one thread per engine, and the browser's engine runs in its own worker apart from the page. What can interleave is reads and changes, every `await` being a point where something else can run: a commit runs one at a time in its turn; a read doesn't wait for turns, and today takes the field as it stands when it starts and works over it to the end; a pausing read pauses many times, so a commit is far likelier to land in its middle."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZA56","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Snapshots already make that safe, the session found from how a store is opened and written: an opened store holds the table it read at open; blocks are never changed or removed and each is checked against its hash; a write applies its changes to a copy of the table and returns a newly opened store, not touching the old. So a store is a value, and a paused read resumes against the field it began with, every holon it reads that field's version even if three commits landed between: snapshot isolation, falling out of immutability, with no locks."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZA8C","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["What a pausing evaluator would have to change: state shared between reads, since today's evaluator is cached per world and keeps one mutable nesting-depth counter, harmless when reads never interleave but with pausing two reads over the same world would corrupt each other's limit, so that counter and anything like it must belong to each read (the session's prototype has no such counter); holons fetched while a read is paused must be kept with the store value they came from, as `field.ts` already keeps a store's holons, never in a cache a later field shares; and an answer reflects its starting field, not the latest, which serving already handles, a subscription read again after every change and a stale answer dropped by its turn counter, so a slow read only means one extra round."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZACM","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Across processes, writes are already serialised outside the engine: one engine writes a store, exclusive locks taken in stores' order, a stale lock from a crash taken over, ref and open volume replaced whole, never overwritten in place. Reads need no locks, reading a hash-checked snapshot."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZAE5","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["One real gap: a store moved by another process. A read started before the move finishes over the old store, which is fine, but the old store's blocks must still exist on disk. Glass never deletes blocks; a `git checkout` could, and then the read fails cleanly on a missing block or hash mismatch rather than returning a mixed answer."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZAG4","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["So pausing adds no hazard the store's design doesn't already cover, but pushes one discipline through the evaluator: what belongs to a read lives in the read. The session will put that in the evaluation with the numbers."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZAHD","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Sorry but i thought a generator based was 1000x faster in some measurement, my thread utilisation and safety was in regards to that"],"typed":"Sorry but i tohught a generator based was 1000x faster in some measurement, my thread utilisaztion and safety was in regards to that"},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZAJ7","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The 1000× is real but comes neither from the generator being faster nor from threads. For `@\"The wiki\".name` on a cold field, today took 1,084 ms because the engine first loads every attached store whole (about 140,000 holons); pausing took 1 ms, fetching only the two holons it needed. The gain is reading 2 holons instead of 140,000. Step for step the generator is slower: with everything loaded, `text(@\"The wiki\")` 348 against 520 ms and `outline(@\"The wiki\", 2)` 517 against 766 ms (1.5× slower), `type(…) | filter(…)` 17 against 16 ms. So pausing wins about 1000× on small cold reads, most of the interface's; today wins about 1.5× on big loaded reads; reads that go up or across (\"what refers to x\", `everything`, `find`, `type`) still need stores whole or the index."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZAPE","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Neither evaluator uses more than one thread. Today's load reads stores concurrently but parses on one thread. The pausing one waits per missing holon in turn; `text(@\"The wiki\")` reads about 48,000 holons, so over a network a round trip each would be far slower than loading whole. The fix is batching, not threads: ask for a fanned-out layer at once, as `readWhole` already does with `Promise.all`."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZARN","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Since speed comes from reading less, the safety answer stands: a read pausing while commits land is safe because a store is an immutable value, provided each read keeps its own state, which today's per-world depth counter doesn't. The cold run of `text(@\"The wiki\")` was still going."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZAT6","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Yes but i wants something super resilient based on strong decision early in the architecture, we are an operating system, nothing can fail and all consumers must be unconditionally served"],"typed":"Yes but i wants something super rescilient basewd on strong decision early in the architecture, we are an operating system, nothing can fail and all consumers must be unconditionally served"},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZAV7","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Then the question is who holds the thread. Today a read runs to its end: `text(@\"The wiki\")` holds the engine's one thread about 350 ms while page, command line, subscriptions and commits wait; a looping read holds it until the depth counter throws; nothing cancels a read whose consumer left. That is structural, not tunable."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZAX2","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session proposed as the early decision that the evaluator be a process the engine schedules: each read a resumable run yielding when it lacks a holon or store (fetched batched by layer), when its time slice is spent (preemption, as Erlang counts reductions), or when ended with a value or stated problem. That buys fairness, cancellation, bounds instead of crashes (the depth counter goes), isolation (runs share only immutable snapshots, which it checked), the 1000× laziness for free, and later threads without changing the evaluator."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZAZV","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["\"Nothing can fail\" honestly means every failure contained, stated, never silent: every consumer gets a value or stated problem within a bound, never a hang or mixed snapshot. Writes already meet that; reads don't."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZB10","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The cost is about 1.5× per step, paid either by generators (about 160 lines; every native that calls back, lists, lazy, sets, find, follow, must learn to pause) or, its leaning, an explicit machine whose stack is plain data, so a paused run is a value to inspect, budget or move between workers, usually as fast in JavaScript, and like the field, state as data. The by-identity path would fold in, ending the duplicate in `field.ts` and `answer.ts`, throw-and-rerun would go, and stores would be loaded whole only when a read goes up or across. It recommended deciding now, then prototyping the explicit machine beside the generator and measuring cost per step, fairness (a small read during `text(@\"The wiki\")`, about 350 ms today, about 1 ms hoped), cold small and cold large reads with batching; it asked whether to write it into \"Glass, as designed\" or see numbers first."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZB5R","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I don't know. I think that there is very little in being concurrent that is blocking. It's really only if you do something that truly changes, right? So there is a minimal thing that you must have the utter respect for in terms of blocking. But there's so much that doesn't need to be blocked to me. And if what you read from is something which is readily available, be it addressed in memory or whatever, I don't quite see why things should be synchronous. And if the indexing and sharing of that and the allowance of using indexing and then having that allowance for concurrence. Then again, if something is blocking, it's down to the performance of the machine, not the convolution of the framework."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZBA0","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session agreed: its scheduler was framework doing the machine's job. Only change needs exclusive respect; everything else reads immutable values, and such readers never need to wait for each other."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZBB3","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["What blocks is one thread, not synchronous code: `async` adds no parallelism, and a generator only lets reads take turns on that thread. The way out is more threads each running the plain synchronous evaluator, limited by cores."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZBCC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The real early decision is that what is read be shareable between threads. Workers share bytes, not objects, and today's world and index are objects, so each worker would reload and rebuild everything. So the field as read should be immutable bytes in shared memory: blocks already are, the table and index laid out as bytes once, any number of readers with no copy or lock. The evaluator then stays synchronous: \"readily available, addressed in memory\" is a plain lookup, with no Lack or rerun; waiting for disk or network is one thread sleeping( `Atomics.wait` ),the machine's wait. One writer per store, a reader pool as large as the cores, shared bytes beneath; no scheduler, generators or throw-and-rerun, and by-identity and whole-store reads become one."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZBGF","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Costs: the index and table must be designed as byte layouts (the open thread \"How the index work is done\"); browsers allow shared memory only on cross-origin isolated pages, needing two headers wherever glass is published; holons are decoded on demand, a different style for natives. It recommended keeping the evaluator simple, not building the generator, and first measuring a few reader workers against one thread (a small read's cost during a big one, throughput with N readers), even with per-worker copies. The generator measure would be stopped."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZBKB","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I do not know why one commits at a time is needed either, honestly. A commit may be run as far as it can, until it's evident that it cannot continue. For something else takes its place, but a lot of commits will never impede on each other. So again, I don't know. Do they need to be one at a time? Yeah, my point is naturally not to be in one thread. Of course not."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZBP0","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["No; what is exclusive can shrink to almost nothing. Reading what a change touches( `known.ts` ),planning (residence, roots, minting), judging, and writing content-addressed blocks are all non-exclusive. Only writing the log line and moving the head is, since the log is a chain: a compare-and-swap per store, microseconds, with all before it in parallel."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZBQV","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Different stores never meet. In one store, a commit whose swap fails because the head moved checks whether what landed touched what its judgment depended on; if not, it re-links and swaps, nothing redone. A real overlap is judged again over the store as it stands and lands or is refused, the author's \"run as far as it can\". Subtly, a rule like \"nothing that stays refers to what goes\" depends on who refers to X, so the check at the swap must be the rules again over what moved, cheap since judging looks only at the landed holons."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZBV3","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Much exists: `known.ts` is the read set, commits already replan when their store moved, locks are taken in stores' order. To change: the engine's single turn goes; minted ids stay unique by an atomic counter or a per-thread part; an act across stores swaps in stores' order, all or none, with \"finished or refused\" if cut short; between processes the swap is a microsecond lock, in the browser one storage transaction. Together: reading shared and immutable on many threads, writing optimistic with one store's head the only exclusive moment, conflicts found and stated, never queued. Neither was measured; the session would keep them as proposals and asked whether to write them so into \"Glass, as designed\"."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZBYW","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I'm not sure that's entirely right. A right in its body, right, already holds. What it does. Is that enough to compare something's blocking? And do you really need to do the full work only to by the end realize if a batch has to be redone though? Not saying that the bodies are enough, but maybe that's not the most brilliant thing either though, is it? And for different stores, it can still matter because something may still be connected, touched, right? And with that, I want to say as well that I want the code to be very well written. Now, what does that mean? Very well written. It means that we draw the components that make the system. In dialogue first. Explaining therefore ontologically what the components are. Explained by whatever it may be. That differentiates them and their roles. Or purposes. That's how we settle before implementation."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZC46","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session stopped the generator measure and agreed with all three points. First, a change's bodies say what it reaches before any work: the holons it writes, lets go and refers to, and the types it instances, since moulds judge it. Two changes meet only where one writes or lets go what the other reaches, decidable on arrival by comparing ids; meeting changes are ordered at once, the later running on top of the earlier, and \"who refers to X\" falls out. Bodies alone aren't quite enough, as moulds and a store's admitted types bear on judging, hence types in reach. Second, ordering first removes doing the work only to redo it. Third, the unit of meeting is the holon, not the store, since holons refer across stores and ids are unique; stores matter only where heads move."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZC8R","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Taking the bar of drawing components in dialogue, each by what it is and what sets it apart, before code, it gave a first drawing: store (a history of acts and a head), snapshot (stores at a moment read as one, immutable, shareable), reader (works an expression over a snapshot, many, none waiting), change (versions and what goes), reach (what a change touches, from the change itself; the word it was least sure of), order (where reaches meet, the only place changes wait), judgment (rules over the snapshot plus what was ordered before), act (a judged change written, moving a head the only exclusive moment). It asked whether reach is its own thing, whether order is a component or a property of how acts link, and whether snapshot is just what field means."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZCD0","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Does that mean, given that there is some state needed, because everybody needs to exist, somewhere for comparison, that we're actually placing the commit in the very field itself? Now, it's actually not in the field, if we think about it. It's stored. Perhaps not. Perhaps we do not want to touch the store. Does that mean we need a stateful engine? And what does that mean?"]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZCFC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Yes, the engine is stateful, and already was (its field, attachments, listeners, what it read); that state was never named. Two kinds: what is, the stores, durable truth, nothing true until an act moves a head; and what is happening, changes in flight with reach and order, reads, subscriptions, attachments, all transient, nothing true lost if the engine dies (an act cut short between heads the store already handles). Changes in flight don't belong in the store, which would stop being \"blocks and a log, no other copy\"; they belong in engine memory, enough because one engine writes a store and so sees every change to compare."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZCJW","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The commit sits, in a sense, in a field not yet true: a later meeting change is judged over the snapshot plus what was ordered before it. So two fields: as it stands (acts only, what readers read) and as it will be (plus changes in flight, what changes are judged against)."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZCMK","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The engine could hold its state as a store in memory, using the existing memory integration: changes in flight, reads, subscriptions as holons, never on disk or mixed with truth, readable in the language as an OS shows processes as files, as \"everything is a holon\" asks. The alternative, a private map in code, is simpler but invisible to the field. It recommended the former, open as to whether now or once programs run, held the what is/ what is happening distinction worth settling regardless, and asked whether it holds or is one step too clever."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZCQQ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["a I get the interpretation. I haven't read the second prompt or the last prompt on, so I'm reading the one before. And when you speak about the reader, you speak about the reader as if it is a unit. That makes me wary. Because is anything really supposed to be a unit? Because then it's a point of failure. Right? It's rather we make components that are virtualized run concurrent with other runs naturally. Just holds and utilizes. I get that you've broken down into these store, snapshot, reader, change, read, but it's not the unit of a machine. It's not the unit of the governing engine. So you're slicing building ontology around something different. And perhaps the components that I were expecting. You're saying that it holds the field, so that means that the field is virtual, within it that is in its memory along with the indexes it builds, so it never actually stands on the stores. Maybe that's what you mean. I'm not sure if I like that. That's interesting. I don't know, I'm skeptical towards something. The idea of using a memory store as then ultimately that is for the engine state machine. That means that you can plug it with anything you want, and that means that you can always introspect that store separately if you want to look at the innards of the engine. That's great, it's pure, but once I interpret it from my read before is that it's anyway already holding the whole field in memory. So that confuses me there."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZD0K","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session agreed it had sliced wrong. \"The reader\" named a role as a thing, inviting a unit that has a count and can fall over. A read is an activity happening wherever there is room; concurrency is what pure work over immutable values gets free, not a component. Draw what the engine does and holds; threads and workers are not ontology."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZD2Q","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["It had conflated two things in memory. The field as read: each store read whole and indexed, the decision the author settled as \"memory could work for now\", only a reading, never a replacement; acts go to the store first and the next field is read from it, so memory can be thrown away and reread, and the engine always stands on the stores. What is happening: changes in flight, reads, subscriptions, with memory as its only home, not needed after a crash. The memory store it suggested is for the second only."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZD5S","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Redrawn around work: stores via integrations, the truth; the field, stores read as one with indexes, derived, disposable, shared; reading, pure, anywhere; changing, judged over the field and the field as it will be, then written as an act, writing the one effect; the engine's state, apart from the field, perhaps a store in memory anyone can look into; serving, the engine carried to whoever asks. It asked if that was nearer the expected components."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZD87","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Just also confirm that I used runs here when that's the terminology in OpenLight for a run of a program. But of course now we're talking about something else, so it's not really a run. Maybe it is a thread, though? Maybe that's exactly what it is. Kind of like container spawned. So the engine loads everything from the stores and then it holds it. I'm wondering if that's an ick for me, because that means again that there can always only be one engine. There can never be multiple engines. Yet we know that the store is an integration. So does that mean that you can run your own integration against a static store such as the files? But there could also be an integration of a store running live somewhere. That your engine would connect to. Because really what happens if the store changes? Now in our picture it shouldn't, because there should only be one engine, but that's low. That's lowballing it."],"typed":"Just also confirm that I used runs here when that's the terminology in OpenAI for a run of a program. But of course now we're talking about something else, so it's not really a run. Maybe it is a thread, though? Maybe that's exactly what it is. Kind of like container spawned. So the engine loads everything from the stores and then it holds it. I'm wondering if that's an ick for me, because that means again that there can always only be one engine. There can never be multiple engines. Yet we know that the store is an integration. So does that mean that you can run your own integration against a static store such as the files? But there could also be an integration of a store running live somewhere. That your engine would connect to. Because really what happens if the store changes? Now in our picture it shouldn't, because there should only be one engine, but that's low. That's lowballing it."},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZDDX","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["\"Run\" belongs to programs (logged, may commit), so a read won't be called one. \"Thread\" fits the machine meaning, but in glass it already names open questions( `type(@types/thread)`, `author/thread.ts` ),and one word with two meanings is forbidden; so a read and a change, in progress or done, with a fresh word if \"a read in progress\" needs one."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZDFW","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["On one engine the author is right: \"one engine writes a store\" put uniqueness in the wrong place. What must be unique is each store's head; arbitrate there and engines can be many. A live store (\"The store as a process\") moves its own head, accepting offers from any engine by compare-and-swap or saying what moved; for static files each engine's integration takes a microsecond lock for the swap. When a store moves, an engine reads the log tail since its known head and patches its reading, as the design already says for outside moves; a change finding the head moved checks the landed acts' written and gone ids against its reach and re-links, or is judged again. Within an engine reach compares early; between engines the head is where they meet."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZDM6","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["So stores have one truth and head each, engines are any number holding readings kept current by following heads, integrations lie between (files, live stores, memory, git, buffers). This would replace \"one engine writes a store; the cli mounts to the host\" (settled 2026-11-12) with \"one head per store, arbitrated where the store lives; engines are many\", letting the cli run its own engine. It asked whether to record that as a moved decision."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZDPM","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["But I still think that any integration now it's starting to lose its name integration, which is to some degree right, but maybe it's hopes I might be wrong. But maybe really integrations were never processes held by the engine exactly. Now again, no, I might be wrong. It might be held, but the only thing it would be the general holding. Because if a store is to be its own, the store as a process, as you would say, then there's nothing that couples it now. If you want to run an integration as a program, on the engine, for whatever the engine interfaces for as its runnable medium, be that a VM or whatever, that is fine. But there is nothing really coupling to it. It's really just a program, an executable that may be executed, through the engine, or may be executed elsewhere. And if it's possible to run separate things in the browser, if it be WebAssembly or workers, then you could run each store, you could set up a store, run it, by yourself if you wanted. Then you can run an engine and connect it to that, and that's fine. Or you could run two engines that share a store if that's what you do. Don't know if there is ever a purpose for that, if that's an anti-pattern or not, I don't know, but I like open-endedness. But again, in the browser, if it's possible to have such threads or multiple workers or WebAssembly things or whatever, of course it might make sense to just start an engine that governs running there. So programs could actually be run in that way, contained in the browser, and then you're going to want to run your integrations through the engine anyway. But still, because that's also it's not peer-to-peer, that's another thing. But it's still, enables live things, which is absolutely crucial. We want to build synthesizers upon the system, all kinds of things. Entering video, audio, DSP, live, whatever. The peer aspect's interesting though, that I say it. What does that mean? And is that something we should consider now anyway? Probably."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZE2J","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session agreed: an integration was never held by the engine but is a program speaking the store's protocol; the protocol and a connection couple store and engine, not ownership. A store is any program speaking it (files, live service, browser worker, wasm); an engine connects to stores and governs over them without containing them; where something is hosted is incidental. It named Plan 9 as a voice: every resource a server on one protocol, mounted the same from anywhere; two engines on one store are two connections, not forbidden."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZE5H","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Live things are where earlier decisions pay: an audio callback has milliseconds and must never wait on a lock or turn, so \"nothing blocks except the machine\" with shared immutable bytes is what real time needs (in the browser an AudioWorklet over shared memory). A stream isn't a history (48,000 samples a second aren't 48,000 acts); some may be committed, a recording or preset, but the stream isn't logged, so \"Streams as integrations\" becomes a source of the same protocol keeping no history."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZE87","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Peers: with each head arbitrated at its store, sharing is client and server per store; peer-to-peer means several peers writing one store without a single head, histories branching and joining as in git. Don't build it, don't preclude it, by two cheap choices now: acts known by content (already so, the same hash everywhere) and acts able to name more than one predecessor, making history a graph, with the settled \"later act wins, marked diverged\" as the seed of joining; today's single predecessor would otherwise mean a format change later. Discovery, sync and trust can wait. It proposed writing these as thinking in motion on the threads \"The store as a process\", \"Streams as integrations\" and \"Peers, real time and shared knowledge\", with \"one engine writes a store\" marked moved, and asked whether to."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZECJ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Is it terminologically, like ontologically correct to say that a store is the gate to a storage? Because I often think of the files as the store, but if we're creating, if the program now is the store, is it then correct to say that the files in that example of the store is then storage? And maybe we'll actually just call our store. Maybe we shouldn't call it file, because that might be thinking that you get a file system projected as holograms. So that's probably wrong. Maybe it's then an object. No, that's also, I don't know, we'll consider. But then again, the store might have multiple storages. Or is it then just separate programs? If it's S3 based or something else, I don't know. What is plan 9? Before you write into the field, I want you to present me. And can you do so with variant? Because if you're to settle for one presentation of the system, built upon it, ontological component, then without you being able to tweak, try other things, other schools of thought, and compare, and how can I feel secure in what you present? Well, that's the next step. Go ahead and describe the components that would make such a system, and that is what we will translate into code."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZEKY","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Store and storage is right: a store is the keeper of a history (holons, acts, a head), a program answering the protocol; storage is where bytes rest (folder, IndexedDB, S3, memory) and knows nothing of holons. The files are storage. A store may rest on several storages, its own business; separate stores exist where histories are separate. Storage needs only immutable blobs by hash and one pointer swappable if unchanged, which Git, IPFS and S3 (conditional writes since 2024) give. The code's `Files` should be called storage, its contents blobs and the head, since \"file\" suggests a file system projected into the field."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZEQC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Plan 9 is Bell Labs' operating system after Unix, from the late 1980s: every resource a server speaking one protocol (9P), presented as a small tree; each process its own namespace mounting servers local or remote alike; `/proc` showing processes as files, like engine state as a readable store. It lost the market but lives on in `/proc`, FUSE, container namespaces and Go."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZESE","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session then drew the whole system four ways. Plan 9: everything a server; utterly uniform, but silent on history, judgment and concurrency. Datomic (Rich Hickey): dumb storage, one transactor per database ordering writes, peers holding the index as a value and told of acts; the clearest split of reading from changing and nearly what was drawn, but a single write point and no peers. Erlang: supervised processes, failure contained and restarted, built for the author's \"nothing can fail\", but state behind messages fits a field read as one value poorly. Git, local-first: every engine a replica, acts a graph, merges; peers by nature, but joins cost everyone. A table compared them: no single point of failure for Plan 9, Erlang (by restart) and Git, while Datomic's writes meet at one point; reads never blocked in Datomic and Git, while Erlang's messages queue; real time natural to Plan 9 and Erlang, not to Datomic or Git; peers natural to Git, permitted by Plan 9's protocol, between nodes in Erlang, absent in Datomic; a trustworthy history in Datomic and Git (with merges), unaddressed by Plan 9 and Erlang; simplicity very high for Plan 9, high for Datomic, medium for the others; closeness to what exists very close for Datomic, the protocol for Plan 9, the format for Git, far for Erlang."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZF0H","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["It proposed a synthesis, Datomic's spine, Plan 9's boundary, Erlang's containment, Git's two precautions: holon, unchanged; storage; store, a program whose head move is the only exclusive point (Datomic's transactor per store, portable); stream, values over time without acts; act, content-known, possibly naming several predecessors; engine, any number, mounting, reading, judging, offering, running programs (Plan 9's namespace, Datomic's peer); field, a disposable reading kept current by following heads, never the truth; read, pure; change, carrying its reach; judgment, pure over moulds, admits and rules; program, run anywhere, logged, supervised and contained, with integrations being programs that are stores or streams (git's reader a store admitting no writes, a microphone a stream); protocol, the one contract (Plan 9's point); the engine's state, a store in memory anyone can read( `/proc` );interface, unchanged. Against what is settled: integration dissolves, one engine per store gives way to one head per store, the format gains a list of predecessors, `Files` becomes storage. Unsure: whether stream and store are one kind with optional history, whether the protocol is one or two (engine-store and consumer-engine; OpenLight had one), whether reach earns a name. Nothing written to the field yet."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZF6Q","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Well, store always has commits. I don't know again if streams are the right name for them, but buffers are what they are, right? And buffers they don't have commit. They don't. Commits integrate across everywhere. Commit can be into stores, etc., and so on and so on. Buffer doesn't have that. But it still operates as such if you create a buffer, then of course some kind of hold on needs to hold that and hold that commit, etc. It's interesting how you went into thinking if you would build something like it, how would you do it in the four different schools? I would have thought that you would not think about that, think about the kind of components you would assemble to build the engine. And the outer parts we now describe, such as a store on its own. And then consider through different schools what those pieces would be. But you've so to some degree you lost something, but you also went a bit wild and perhaps earned a few thoughts. You are saying protocol. The contract between all above. That's interesting. I never thought of that, for instance."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZFDB","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Buffer, not stream: a value now, without commits; commits integrate across everything, and a commit referring to a buffer needs something to hold it, as \"Lifetime as a contract\" leans (depend on what may vanish by its declared lifetime). The session admitted it had built the system four times when it should have drawn components first and held each up to the schools, and did that now. The one protocol it credited to OpenLight, kept under \"Where it comes from\", for the engine alone; new is stores and buffers speaking it."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZFGB","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Outer. Storage: blobs by hash and a head (Datomic and Git alike; Unix's file system says too much, the \"file\" the author distrusted); picked as the whole contract. Store: a keeper of history, always with commits (Datomic's transactor, a Plan 9 file server, an Erlang process, a Git repository); picked as a program holding a head, its move the only exclusive moment. Buffer: a value of the moment living by declared lifetime (a Unix pipe, an Erlang process's state, a DSP ring buffer never waited on, an FP value held by what refers to it); picked as held by its referrers under its lifetime, remade if it dies, nothing pretending otherwise. Commit: one content-known act into every store it touches (Datomic's transaction, Git's multi-parent commit, two-phase commit); picked as today plus room for several predecessors. Program: runs anywhere, logged, may offer commits; Erlang's containment picked. Protocol: one contract (Plan 9, OpenLight, Unix pipes); open whether one or two sharing a grammar, leaning one."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZFNP","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Inner. Mount: a namespace (Plan 9) and OpenLight's attach record, these stores here. Field: weighed against an FP persistent value sharing what didn't change and a Git checkout, Datomic's database as a value picked, derived, following heads, never the truth. Index: open whether the engine's (in memory) or the store's (shared blobs, Datomic's choice), the thread \"How the index work is done\", mattering most for many engines and threads. Language: as is, code as data (Lisp). Evaluator: synchronous and pure, the generator let go since laziness comes from where data lives. Names: resolved within what is mounted, a shared name refused with each holon named (Plan 9 names per namespace). Judgment: pure over field and commit (FP), while in Datomic judgment sits where the head is (the transactor checks); open whether in engine, store or both, a store judging what it admits and the engine what the field declares across stores. Committing: reach first (the author's point: known from the bodies, so meeting changes are ordered before any work and nothing is thrown away), order where reaches meet, the head the only exclusive moment, against optimistic check-at-the-end. State: a store in memory or a buffer anyone can look into( `/proc` ). Serving: as is, now the same protocol. Ranked open questions: index in engine or store, one protocol or two, where judgment happens, whether state is a store or (probably) a buffer. It asked which first."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZFXG","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I'll also add now before I read the components because you say them that it is. Stores. Buffers and the engine all need to speak. Well, we do know that the engine and interfaces need to speak. And we do know that whatever hosts programs needs to speak. So there is a bit of unit and they need to speak. Now, of course, upon the engine, you're already in a field there is already that is already resolved, but now we're talking outside of that, and that's when you get to infrastructure, etc. So stores and buffers needs to be subscribable for the engine, right? Otherwise nothing works. And there is a subscribable connection between the interface and the engine. The relationship between a medium to run programs in, I don't know. But most likely it means running something of the engine, right? In there, that's also opens that kind of connection. So we're talking about a two-way connection, is my feeling. And how do we do that? How do we do that in an open-ended way? For holding in infrastructure. But also considering that you might want to run a lot of different stores and so if you do it through the engine then you run it on whatever is the connection to where the programs are on that kind of outlet. It's interesting. So this obsession with running containers, which makes sense, when operating systems are so unsafe as they are treated. I really don't know about existing operating systems that processes the nature of things there, how safe processes are from each other, etc. Or at least we need to think here just about the future. A wee bit. But audio and DSP and these things being brought in, that's definitely part of it. But I think it's any area of compute where naturally this is to be workable. Alright, very interesting progression in this session. I actually didn't read all of these because okay, you had the outer first, interesting. Alright. I skimmed through the outer a little bit. Components you've broken apart in the inner I'm not sure they are the components that actually map into functions and files. So it might be a bit again rudimentary slicing."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZGA6","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The connection is what every piece shares: engine to store or buffer, interface to engine, engine to a medium running programs (which likely runs a piece of an engine). So the primitive is a symmetric two-way connection where either side can ask and be answered, subscribe and be told on change, and offer a commit and hear if accepted, over any carrier (message port, socket, pipe, network). Many stores are many connections; where a store runs is just the carrier. Live compute adds a second channel, shared bytes in a ring neither side waits on: messages say what to do, bytes carry the signal."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZGDM","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Isolation: today's operating systems isolate memory but not authority, so containers remove ambient reach after the fact. Capabilities answer instead: reach only what you were handed a connection to, as in Fuchsia (channels carrying handles), WebAssembly's component model, and in part Plan 9's namespaces. For glass a program reaches only the connections it is given, governance as what you hand, matching \"what a component may read is what it was given\"."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZGFZ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The author was right that the inner slicing was concepts, not code. A first file drawing: in `engine/`, `engine.ts` (verbs and map), `connection.ts`, `mount.ts` (connections by name, heads followed), `field.ts` (made anew from each store's tail), `read/` ( `evaluate.ts`, `natives/`, `index/` ), `change/` ( `reach.ts`, `order.ts`, `judge.ts`, `offer.ts` ), `state.ts` (readable as a buffer); outside, `store/`, `storage/`, `buffer/`. New: `connection.ts` as the heart, absorbing today's serving, protocol and worker pieces; stores connected to rather than opened; change drawn by reach and order instead of known and plan; storage its own place. Unsure of the line between mount and field, and where a store run by an engine belongs. It offered to draw it file by file and hold each up to the schools before writing."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZGMC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I think you're falling short. You're lowballing my prompt. I mentioned this connection that's evidently needed between different modules, but it's about the infra, the whole stuff. Yes, you're saying that that is cross or things. Okay, interesting. Does that mean that we're not looking at reusing some existing standard or invention? You're saying that it's ultimately such a simple concept that we don't need to settle for how it's done. You're good that you understand that the components weren't sliced, right? But I'm not looking exactly at files either. Or we may have files, but we may have shitty insides of the files. So I'm not asking for files, I'm asking for system thinking. That has more to do with functions, closures, invocations of something that in our return invocates, etc., etc. Now you're placing connection internally now. Of course, since the engine does connection, something needs to sit there, but I'm still asking, are we talking about something a tool outside of the engine that the others also used or utilize or not? I do not know yet. I'm also heavily OCD. When it comes to designing systems like this, maybe it's right though to put things in read and in change. Sometimes you build similar things and in terms of that they all hold A, B and C, A, B and C, even though they're all different. Like feature-based structuring, as an example. And sometimes you do another way. I'm not sure if read and change is exactly what it is, but we don't need to focus on negatives here. Let's proceed instead with what is inviting in my prompt."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZGXN","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session said it had made the connection a module of the engine when the author meant the medium everything lives in, and took \"functions, closures, invocations that in return invoke\" as the school of object capabilities (E in the 1990s, Cap'n Proto, Cap'n Web): a connection is functions callable on the other side; following is handing over a closure( `follow(told)` returning `stop` );holding a function is authority, with no names or ambient access; where it runs is invisible. So the connection is one tool outside every party."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZH0H","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["On reusing a standard it compared Cap'n Web (Cloudflare, 2025: JavaScript-native, under 10 kB, no schemas, two-way, functions by reference; closest), Cap'n Proto RPC (its heavier, schema'd parent), Comlink (worker proxies only), JSON-RPC and LSP (no references, so no capabilities), gRPC (heavy, server-shaped), 9P (the spirit, not the means) and Fuchsia FIDL (OS-level). It proposed committing to the model and trying Cap'n Web as carrier, noting its launch post calls it experimental and a third party finds its typing thin, with an own small one as fallback; glass's expressions already send whole computations, so reads gain less from chaining than engine-to-store traffic."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZH3T","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["It sketched parties as offerings: storage `blob`, `put`, `head`, `swap`; `store(storage)` offering `head`, `acts(since)`, `follow`, and `offer(act)` (accepted or moved, the one exclusive moment); a buffer with `now`, `write`, `follow`, `lifetime`; `engine()` with `mount`, `read`, `follow`, `commit`; a program as a function from given capabilities to offerings, run by a host; `chassis(engine)`. Consequences: everything composes alike, local or remote only the carrier; authority is what was handed, so narrowing is mounting less, settling the worry in `engine.ts` that \"no wrapper can narrow what an expression reaches\"; one `follow(told) → stop` shape from disk to page; a store run by an engine or elsewhere is the same thing handed differently; audio flows through shared bytes handed once. Its loose end: could an engine be mounted into another, federation, or is that a confusion of kinds? It proposed a scratch prototype (store in a worker, engine in the page, interface following a read, over Cap'n Web), citing Cap'n Web's repository and README, Cloudflare's launch post and Lumenize's comparison."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZH9H","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
