{"body":{"text":["`atHand` is the held field: what the engine has read of its mounted stores, in memory, and so should be called field; the session had avoided the word only because of today's field object with methods. `inFlight` is the engine's only state for concurrency, read by `order`. `takeIn` is the other direction from putting to a store: offer is engine to store (\"put this act into your history\"), takeIn store to engine (\"this act is now in my history\"), put into the held field."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZKV5","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["On subscription and performance: it carries only change (each act names what it wrote and let go; contents are blocks read if needed), so cost follows how much changes, never store size; one cannot stay in sync more cheaply than the change itself. Databases reached the same: replicas following the primary's log, Datomic's peers sent each new act, `git fetch` bringing only missing objects. What costs is the first reading at mount, about half a second locally for everything."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZKXQ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Watertight, because each act names the one before: subscribe from the head held (\"I have everything up to H\"); the store sends every act since H, then live; a missing act is visible at once and asked for again from the last head; reconnecting is subscribing from the last head; if history is rewritten (checkout, pull) and H is gone, the store says so and the engine reads afresh; duplicates are no-ops. The log followed with a cursor; the format already carries it."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZM0D","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Where the holding is done: (a) in each engine's memory, read on mount, today: simple, but each engine pays the first reading and rebuilds the index; (b) as a store of its own, the designated store explored before: the held field and index at a head H kept as blobs, giving fast start (fetch the reading at H, subscribe from H: snapshot plus log, as a database starts a replica; glass's log is halfway there, every volume opening with the whole table), shared and swappable among engines, threads and workers, and introspectable. The tension in (b) is the author's principle of each fact one home: a stored holding is a copy, honouring it only if marked derived at H, never truth, recomputable, discardable: a cache."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZM4D","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Recommendation: subscribe by following the log with a cursor now; hold in memory now; when a first reading costs (remote stores, many engines, the browser), store the holding derived at a head, the index first as costliest and most worth sharing, which is the open thread \"How the index work is done\"."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZM63","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Water tightness I spoke of was about the connection between engine and store, etc. I was just doing some general critique against that connection. I must say I really like how we're drawing the commits here. In terms of tone of voice, in terms of style, going into the main file of the engine, seeing such briefly orchestrated verbs is wonderful. So I think, and overall this session has had a lot of value. We've come really far, so let's proceed here. I am a little concerned here, and we are talking, you now recommending things. And I'm just still being skeptical. Is it so that the reasonable and the right thing to do is that each store is its own and it communicates through a kind of subscription relationship to whatever engines connect to them? And the engine has to read the stores and hold it in memory because otherwise you'd have to fire a request immediately. So you want to bring the data close to you, cache it, so to say. And then you want to build indexes upon them. And then the engine is ready to do the action close to where it is and fast. Bare bones, bare metal, so to say. We spoke about some degree of queuing things, but otherwise, if they do not touch each other, things are just welcome to read. So that what we hold in memory then is open. Now, what I am a little bit wary of is it that simple that when we look at the engine, the field is just a variable in the closure of the file? That everything just reads from. Or do we have to go more object-oriented that there is an actual unit that holds it and that you need to ask from it"]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZMG5","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The author's summary is right and the simple version: each store is its own; engines subscribe to the stores they connect to; an engine reads each store once, holds it close, builds indexes on it, and works next to its data, bare metal. Changes wait only for changes they meet; reads never wait. Two qualifications: the holding is a cache, the store the truth; holding everything works at our size (137,000 holons), and a store too big for memory would be held in part and read on a miss with the same functions over a partly filled field, not needed now."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZMKF","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The field as a variable in the engine's closure is right, not a shortcut, on one condition: the field is a value never changed in place. `let field = empty`; `read: (expression) => evaluate(expression, field)`; `takeIn: (act) => { field = next(field, act); answer(field); }`, the only place it is replaced. A read begun before a commit finishes over the field it started with; no asking, no locking, no unit. This is Clojure's answer, Rich Hickey's \"values, not places\": the identity is a reference, its state a series of immutable values. An object you ask hides data behind behaviour, makes readers depend on its being alive and consistent at the moment of asking, and invites change in place, which brings locks; the unit the author is wary of is what letting the field be data avoids."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZMQF","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Today already has `let field` and `become(next)`, but the field carries methods( `holonsOf`, `world()`, `logOf`, hidden loaders); naked it is plain data, loading done in the steps (mount, takeIn). The index made when a pattern first asks is object-like inside a value; either build it in `takeIn` or memoise it beside the value keyed by it; the session leaned to building and patching it in `takeIn`. A closure variable belongs to one engine on one thread; workers each hold their own field until the holding is shared bytes, set aside for now, fine since engines may be many."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZMTQ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["To proceed, the session proposed first writing this session's drawing into the field (the store as its own, subscribed to; the held field as a value in a closure; commit as a journey with refusal as a value; the generator left aside and why), then turning it into the engine."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZMWB","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["But what about threads then? What about is TypeScript multi-thread or single thread? Let's just start with that question. And in the browser,"]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZMX8","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["TypeScript has no threads of its own: it compiles to JavaScript, which runs one thread per context, one call stack and an event loop, so two pieces of code never run at once in one context. One thread is still concurrent: I/O runs in parallel underneath, so many reads can be waiting at once but only one computing."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZMZ6","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Parallelism comes from workers, each a thread with its own heap and event loop: in the browser Web Workers, plus SharedWorker (shared by tabs), Service Worker (between page and network, not for computing), AudioWorklet, and OffscreenCanvas for drawing in a worker; Bun and Node have the same Web Worker API (Node also `worker_threads` ); `navigator.hardwareConcurrency` reports cores; one can start that many, or more."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZN18","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The crux is sharing: objects are never shared, `postMessage` copies them by structured clone, so a field of 137,000 holons sent to a worker is copied holon by holon; a byte buffer can be transferred without copying, the sender losing it; a `SharedArrayBuffer` is seen by both threads, coordinated with `Atomics`. The browser requires cross-origin isolation( `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` and `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp` or `credentialless` ),else `SharedArrayBuffer` doesn't exist, and the main thread may not block with `Atomics.wait`, only workers may. Beyond JavaScript, WebAssembly threads share one wasm memory, and WebGPU computes on the graphics card."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZN49","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["For glass: one engine on one thread, parallelism as several workers, which can share the held field only as bytes. So either each worker holds its own copy (simple, costing memory and the first reading), or the held field is laid out as bytes once and shared by all: the decision that decides how far reading can spread across the machine."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZN69","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Because my whole point about the engine was that the in-memory field we hold is just memory. And then each verb that we handle. A lot of what happens there doesn't need synchronicity. I mean, we have a certain queue system based on the request bodies, but otherwise it's really just like we are ready to spawn threads. For each request, we take in, right? And it just reads the shared memory. But now I'm looking at this and I'm wondering what is what."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZN96","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The author's model holds; JavaScript only forces its shape. The field as just memory becomes bytes in a `SharedArrayBuffer`, since objects can't be shared. A thread per request becomes a pool of workers started once, one per core, a request a message to a free worker; a worker per request would cost milliseconds and a fresh heap each time. Each reads the shared memory and decodes only the holons it touches. The queue by the bodies' reach becomes one coordinating thread holding `inFlight`, ordering commits that meet, everything else passing straight to a worker. The engine's `field` variable becomes, in each worker, only which version it reads, a number."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZNCV","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Bytes are less foreign than they sound: blocks already are bytes, JSON lines, each version a slice. A field as bytes: the blocks' bytes as read; a table of where each id's latest version sits (offset, length) as typed arrays( `Int32Array` );the index's facts as typed arrays. A read finds the id, decodes one slice, touches nothing else."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZNES","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["A change lands without stopping readers because memory is only appended to: `takeIn` writes new versions after the old, publishes a new table and moves the version number with one atomic write; a reader on the old version keeps the old table, whose offsets point at bytes never overwritten. Nobody waits, one place writes: the field as a value done in bytes. Memory grows with every change, so now and then the field is compacted into a fresh buffer and readers move over, the cost of never overwriting."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZNHN","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["What is what: workers are reading threads, one per core; the coordinator is the engine's main thread, subscribing to stores, receiving requests, ordering commits, the only writer to shared memory( `takeIn` );shared memory is the held field (blocks, table, index as bytes); the store stays outside, the truth, followed by subscription. The simpler alternative, each worker holding its own copy as objects as today, needs no byte format but costs memory times workers and pays the first reading and index in each: a fine first step, but not the author's model, since nothing is shared."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZNMQ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["So the real decision is the held field as bytes, more work than objects and what makes \"spawn, read the shared memory, done\" true. A small measure would show whether decoding a holon per read is cheap enough: a few workers over the main store's blocks as bytes against today's one thread over objects."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZNPG","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Well, I mean, most often you work with a snapshot. If you work with history, of course, then you do but I don't know, to me the memory available is the memory you have available and that's how much you designate in a way. If you have infinite you do, but the point is you're talking about compacting perhaps just what is relevant replaces that which is the most stale one in terms of reading. It's my gut feeling for what I want, but so you're saying that workers are threads, or workers available in bun and in dino and Node.js and so on? I like the idea of going to bytes, even though we are in TypeScript, yes. And to say, is it that a thread is per core? I didn't know that. I still don't know these main things about OSS, honestly."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZNVA","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The author's memory-as-designated-budget matches a well-known shape: a cache with a budget evicting what was read least recently. The snapshot lives in it; when full, what has gone longest unread makes room; a miss fetches from the store's blocks, always there; history is fetched only when asked. Compacting becomes keeping what is used and dropping what is stale. The index should not be evicted: holons can be dropped and refetched by id, but \"what refers to X\" or \"everything of type T\" needs facts about every holon, and the index is small beside the bodies, so it stays whole while bodies come and go."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZNYR","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The Web Worker API( `new Worker`, `postMessage`) is a web standard in all modern browsers, Bun and Deno; Node has it not as a global but as `worker_threads`. `SharedArrayBuffer` and `Atomics` exist in all, with the browser's isolation condition. Each worker is a real OS thread."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZP0B","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The OS picture: a core is hardware executing instructions, running one thread at an instant (some two interleaved, Intel's hyper-threading; Apple's M-series has performance and efficiency cores). A thread is a line of execution (position, call stack, registers); the OS holds thousands. The scheduler decides which threads run on which cores, switching many times a second; with more ready threads than cores they take turns. A process is a running program with its own walled-off memory, the OS's isolation; threads live inside a process and share its memory. So threads aren't per core, but only as many run at a moment as there are cores, which is why a compute pool is sized to the cores. Threads that mostly wait cost almost nothing, which is also why one JavaScript thread can keep many I/O requests going. JavaScript's workers are threads in one process, but JavaScript chose not to let them share ordinary memory, so objects are copied and only `SharedArrayBuffer` bytes are shared: the reason behind the field as bytes."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZP5X","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I don't know if it's a kind of push, the oldest out thing. I don't know. What is the difference in speed of a good SSD and memory? What is it that justifies something being moved out or not? I don't know. These are interesting things I never thought about before. Should we also should the engine be runnable in Node 1 and Dino? What is the atomics you refer to here? So the OS can hold thousands of threads because threads pause. Is that so? Okay, we're making lots of good progress. I'm liking this. But there is still some uncertainties out there, but they might not need resolution yet. But honor them as much as you can, as we are closing in on the end,"]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZPAC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Typical figures: RAM about 0.1 µs to read one small thing, tens to hundreds of GB/s; a good NVMe SSD about 20–100 µs, about 3–7 GB/s; a nearby network about 1 ms and up. Memory is roughly a few hundred to a thousand times faster for one small read, ten or more for streaming. Two things blur that: the OS page cache keeps recently read files in spare memory; and for us decoding often costs more than fetching (loading the main store took 257 ms, mostly parsing JSON). What justifies keeping something is the cost of a miss (fetch plus decode) times how often it's asked, against the memory it takes. Oldest out (LRU) is the common default; LFU keeps what is read most often; ARC balances recency and frequency. Which fits need not be settled now: an uncertainty to keep, not resolve."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZPF3","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Node and Deno: the session would make it a principle that the engine runs wherever JavaScript runs, and checked that it is almost true already: nothing in `engine/` or `language/` depends on Bun; Bun appears only in git's integration (spawning `git`, hashing) and in the site's building and hosting. The rule: the engine uses only web standards (workers, `SharedArrayBuffer`, Web Crypto, `fetch`, streams), and what is particular to a runtime (filesystem, process) lives in storage or programs, at the edge."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZPHP","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["`Atomics` is a global for safe operations on shared memory: an atomic operation is seen whole or not at all( `Atomics.store`, `Atomics.load`, `Atomics.add` without lost increments); `Atomics.compareExchange` is the compare-and-swap discussed before; `Atomics.wait` and `Atomics.notify` sleep until a value changes and wake sleepers. In the held field as bytes, `takeIn` writes the new bytes, then one `Atomics.store` moves the version number, and readers `Atomics.load` it, seeing old or new, never a mixture."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZPM3","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Thousands of threads, yes, because most are paused: Activity Monitor shows thousands on 8–16 cores, nearly all blocked on disk, network, a timer or a key; a blocked thread uses no CPU; the scheduler runs only ready ones; each costs a little memory, mainly its stack, reserved generously but paid only as used."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZPNT","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Uncertainties carried: eviction (oldest, least used or balanced, and whether only the index stays whole); the held field as bytes (table and index as typed arrays over blocks, versions published atomically: promising, not measured); engine and store (whether stores are always programs reached by subscription, and how a store run by an engine differs from one run elsewhere); the protocol (one, or two sharing a grammar, and whether capability RPC such as Cap'n Web carries it, a box opened and set aside); an act cut short across stores, where refusal and failure meet, not yet drawn simply; peers (content-known acts, room for more than one predecessor so peers aren't precluded); buffers and lifetime (what holds a buffer a commit refers to); words (whether reach earns a name, field versus held field, takeIn)."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZPT0","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Settled enough to carry, though not ratified: the generator evaluator let go; the evaluator synchronous and pure, its data at hand; commit as a journey; refusal as a value and failure thrown to the offer's edge; the field as a value in the engine's closure, replaced only by `takeIn`; mint after judge, so problems speak in the caller's handles; stores two-way, one path bringing every change in; the engine on web standards only. The session would write it as thinking in motion, not settled, beside \"Glass, as designed\", each uncertainty on its thread, and the decision \"one engine writes a store\" marked as moved, so the next session starts from here and not from compaction."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZPXP","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["thing that lots of time are spent on decoding. Makes me wonder, how should we talk? Or wait, we're no longer decoding because it is only when this subscription to a store happens that we decode. Otherwise, we're already store decode, don't we? I was just thinking if we should actually already talk decoded. Between the stores, but maybe that's irrelevant there. What's in our steps then, in our verbs and their steps? Where is the thread run continuous? And where would it most likely pause out of curiosity? So regarding eviction, and regarding using this byte storage that I've never seen about before, that is available in JavaScript, the shared array buffer. What happens when you hit its limit? How big is it? And how does that work? Because that, to me, answers everything about eviction. Let's not use anything such as cap and web or something like that. We don't need that yet. We just do things simple, and that can be thought about later. But I am questioning, the format in which we will communicate. Is it JSON? Is it bytes? What is it? I don't know. I don't know if we're going to consider the kind of branching that peers are about, because we've done so much progress that a rewriting of the engine, even though we don't think about everything, still places us in a good place. But of course, if something vital can be thought of now and we want to, we should. Yeah, we're going to go over the words a little bit before implementation, but we can always change it when the implementation is done as well."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZQ74","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Today decoding happens once, at mount: blocks parsed into objects, reads then touching objects and never decoding: the 257 ms. With the held field as bytes it moves: bytes held as they came, with a table of where each holon sits, a holon decoded when touched. Mounting costs almost nothing; a read pays a few microseconds per holon touched. Decoding moves from everything up front to only what is read."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZQ9G","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["On format: talk in the format we store in. The blocks are JSON lines, so the same bytes can sit in storage, cross the connection and be held in memory with no translation at any boundary. \"Talking decoded\" doesn't really exist between threads or processes: anything that crosses is bytes, and decoded exists only inside one heap, so the choice is only which bytes. The session said JSON, since the blocks already are it and JavaScript engines parse it very fast; binary later if a measure asks. The one exception is the index, which needs facts from every holon and so decodes everything once, then is kept and patched: the one up-front cost left, and why a stored index derived at a head may be worth it someday."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZQDP","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Where threads run and pause: read runs resolve, evaluate, the index, pausing only on a miss fetched from storage; subscribe runs each answer as a read, idle between changes; commit runs reach, place, judge, mint, write, pausing at order if it meets a change in flight and at offer, the longest, blocks, line and head written to storage, often milliseconds for the disk to confirm; takeIn runs updating field and index and answering subscriptions, pausing to fetch the act's blocks if not held; mount builds the table and the index once, pausing to read the store. Pauses are I/O and waiting on a change that meets yours; everything else is plain computation, which is why it can spread across threads."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZQHH","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["`SharedArrayBuffer` is raw memory of fixed size given at creation, or growable( `new SharedArrayBuffer(bytes, { maxByteLength })`, then `.grow(n)` ),only growing, never shrinking, up to the declared maximum. How big depends on the engine (V8; JavaScriptCore for Safari and Bun; SpiderMonkey); on 64-bit the limits run to gigabytes and browsers cap memory per tab; the session didn't know exact figures and said to measure rather than trust its memory. At the limit nothing happens for you: making or growing throws; nothing is evicted, since the runtime doesn't know what the bytes mean. So the author was right that it answers eviction: the budget is the buffer's size, and eviction is ours to write, our own small allocator. The complication: a reader may still be on an old version when its bytes are about to be reused; the usual answer is epochs, each reader noting its starting version, a region reused only once no reader needs it: simple in principle, the one subtle part of holding bytes ourselves."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZQPT","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Cap'n Web: agreed, set aside. Peers: nothing vital now; acts naming several predecessors would be an added field, old one-predecessor lines staying valid, so the engine can be rewritten without designing for peers and lose nothing. Words: gone over before implementing, free to change after. The offer to write this session's drawing into the field was still open; with the above, the drawing would also carry decoding on touch, one format (JSON) from storage to memory, and the buffer as the budget with eviction ours and epochs the subtle part."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZQSS","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
