{"body":{"text":["The size need not be chosen up front; three ways, simplest to most fitting. 1. Migrate when full: a new larger buffer, everything copied, readers switched; simple, but it pauses while copying and briefly needs twice the memory: fine at the start, poor once big. 2. Reserve high, grow as needed: a growable buffer declares a maximum, but on 64-bit reserving costs almost nothing since the OS hands out real memory only for touched pages; the maximum is a ceiling, not an allocation, so the whole budget could be reserved once at the start whatever is attached later. Growable shared buffers are new, and the session was not sure every runtime we care about supports them yet: to be checked. 3. Segments instead of one buffer, the one the session leaned to: many fixed-size buffers, say 64 MB; when more room is needed a new segment is made and handed to the workers (shared buffers pass between threads); eviction works on whole segments, the oldest's still-used holons copied into the current one and the old let go once no reader is on it (epochs); attaching fixes no size, a store's holons going into the current segment, the budget simply how many segments are allowed."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZR2R","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["What the session liked in the third: it is the store's own shape again in memory. The store is append-only blocks of about 64 KB and log volumes that open anew; the held field would be append-only segments, the oldest compacted into the newest: one pattern at two scales. So the author's \"migrate when we need\" is right, done one segment at a time, continuously and in small pieces. This joins the field's open questions: segments as the held field's shape, their size, what makes the budget. The offer to write the session's drawing into the field still stood."]},"id":"1M78WEQVZBZZR61","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
