{"body":{"text":["Regarding the program's opening and single paragraph, I like to clarify upon my previous statement, quoted as in, a program is a series of thought. If we're to truly acknowledge what thought is, my gut feeling is it is not only rational. There is another element to existence. Though I guess thoughts are in a way trackable. They are in a way systemic. But I wouldn't want to go so far as to say that programs are like a series of thought. They are like, but they are not. The fullness of what thought means. There is an analogy in terms of how they are similar. Certainly. And that goes in the quote. There is always a beginning and an end, and it comes from the field and dies in the field. But I'd like to add my correction there."]},"id":"1M7GH1YB0K10133","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Another correction would be that components do not necessarily need to be defined in code. They can be entirely defined in the field, as layout, as data. This we know. I think we could be a bit more clear. Not having read the second paragraph yet, I'll say out loud what I think about the nature, the ontological meaning of the primal component that we refer to as the component. The field is multidimensional. Functions is a way of escalating that even further. Components, however, they are more strictly holarchical, as in single root, right? That is not the nature of the field. Now, what it does is a component, of course, matches against some kind of interpolation of the field. Now, whatever that interpolation is, for the lack of better terminology, forgive me if that was wrong. We structurally, hierarchically, as is, user interfaces, such as the dormitory. We have a means of taking that interpolation and routing it through the hierarchical nature of the UI, such as a list or whatever. But the point is that however the field is broken down further, and however we continue to interpolate it, more components match by type. So it is a means of taking the multidimensional field, using the archetypal primitives of the components that which they take, and they themselves may serve back and open up for more components to be mounted. It is a very particular drawing and mathematically, systematically, very particular phenomenon. I think overall, many of these briefs that I read here, they just hold a little bit of what I've said, and maybe a little bit of inference between what I've said. I'm not asking you to grow all of the text a lot. I'm not asking you to fill in and add lots of words. But it is my statement that there is room for a lot more powerful words. It is a little bit light, certainly is. These primitives are what is essential to culture as we begin to actually have programs and actually run inference on this field. There's going to be an art to inference in a different way. The culture that we birth for how it reasons is at the same time mirrored in all aspects of its existence. So whilst there is the primitives here, ontologically defined as powerfully as possible, which is not as verbosely as possible, naturally, it linking it to the language of reality, explaining these things, we give different connection points, different facets to the meaning of these things. Combined with, say, a more directed process philosophy, which is, I haven't read yet, and yeah, you've talked about how we work, but that's entirely from you, and it's not anything that I've channeled for you yet so much. Even though it's ultimately what the room started to realize, but never fully embodied. There is process philosophy. There is philosophy about process. So we're, as we birth inference through the field, we may orchestrate the persona, and what I refer to as the culture. So as we found that with strong and powerful words, having these primitives be fully in their power, together with that culture, I think it gives a very strong foundation. It's integration. Right. And we may translate that to other things as to how we work more and more. All of these things connect. So this is of course a vital point of the field we're building, and now we're just doing this knowledge formula, but later everything's gonna interlink. All of the code related to type, all of the things, it becomes an interdimensional mesh. So these briefs right now, they will evolve further into very concentrated, or shall we say, highly interconnected and vital foundational points upon which things rest. Now, the brief structure is more holarchic, the multidimensional, of course. All right, that was a bit of talking."]},"id":"1M7GH1YB0K101RN","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I'd say there is to a certain degree an overuse of verbatim quotes, though I appreciate doing it because while now it's just text, truthfully, you could actually make a quote be a specific thing, right? So it could actually be a whole on and a reference to the very quote itself and thus have its own component that renders it, etc. Let's not do that now. I appreciate it. It's grounding, it's interconnecting. This may be a slight overuse sometimes. For instance, what holds it whole? What is really the point there? There's a lots of quotes, but perhaps what's really to be conveyed perhaps could be honored more clearly. For describing the store, it's quite quick there with something, stating that the store is whole, it contains all the changes. It's really not, it's not quite. I don't know precisely yet how to channel my feedback at that point. I don't know what the home is, and the talk about the home where a person's sovereignty is built. I don't know what that's about. I don't know. I find what it holds whole. No, I find one change end to end, what it holds whole, and who is in it. Not as powerful, so it could be a little bit more fragmented, not entirely right with me. I think we really want to consider what it is we want to convey and give it more structurally defined and purposeful briefs and prose probably. And some of my comments, additional points to what's being talked about here as well. I'm not going into the depth now."]},"id":"1M7GH1YB0K1021P","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session took the author's notes on what he had read: inference on the field no longer linear, every inference of one emanation, transparent and decomposable though not pure; isolation by the engine's choice, never built in; type undertold; the heart never the breath, the symbiosis of human and machine at the top of the gradient; the source perhaps primal, the reverse of the interface; the program like thought and not thought; components definable in the field and strictly holarchical over a many-dimensional field; fewer quotes, and the last sections rethought. Asked to humour him, it read the primitives through the Matrix: the Source and the jack, the Architect as type, the Oracle as the function, the exiles as programs, the reload as erasure, Smith as the unit, Neo as the bridge; it said glass refuses erasing, which he corrected."]},"id":"1M7GH20KN96W04E","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Sounds like you're you're responding well to what I've said. You're hearing what I'm saying. Great. Not that that's how things work, but anyway. Regarding the matrix analogy, isn't the oracle in a way more of a trickster than the wise woman or sage? Is it really only resonance, or isn't there a kind of jokering aspect of it? Isn't the oracle and Smith in a way the same? Glass doesn't refuse erasing. It doesn't. Creating a new store and destroying an old one, certainly possible. And we shall keep in mind that since you can take something out of a store, we might down the line allow that. Taking something out, making it external, therefore removing it, most definitely gonna be needed. All right, or perhaps I just let go of the matrix talk there. We needn't say any more. All right, let's let go of matrix there. And yes, naturally, of course, take all of what I've said, and as you already know, the kind of cycles, practices that are needed to actually reach pristine material. I suspect that you're going to do quite a lot of reasoning cycles, agents, etc. So you know what to do. Just honor what I've said and evolve the knowledge whole on from there. I briefly dwelt into the depth as well. Maybe I think it's a bit fragmented in terms of how things are siloed into their documents there. And again, we wanna evolve organically upon the primitives. It's those that and the philosophy behind them is what really ought to resonate through everything. The wire is one such thing. Um, As I know, there's been references to Plan 9, for instance, and more. The wire is it is also unification, right? It is crucial. It has the same values as the field. It's about and the way it becomes practical is quite an interesting experience. How beautiful code can be, where really it's just function, it's function, but then as soon as you want to, you can take a function and it can be a program. It can be run in an infrastructure however you want to. And you can exchange the adapters for the wire. And the way that ultimately our system needs to cross thresholds as sources are autonomous, and as the engine is, and as the runtime is, and so on. So the wire is a really powerful thing. And then you talk about rounding, but then I think you should also probably consider the depth. I think it loses a certain touch there. All right, good luck."]},"id":"1M7GH22WFDG70EM","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
