{"body":{"text":["333333 Look, we're considering ontology, we're considering primitives, we're considering transparency and honesty for what is what, breaking down into its most simple yet foundational component. Simple yet, shall we say, enabling, right? Maybe we are creating primitives that are somewhat superficial, for there are stronger primitives below them we are not seeing. My point is, the chassis that runs an interface and a source is there a difference in their established connection to the engine? I guess that was my question. Because we know now that it may go both ways. We might just as well want to make changes to the external world. Essentially, it's I/O, you know. And I know that if we look back to before OpenLight, in the strange of AGI, which is the export of my initial discussions with ChatGPT over the weekend, that is the first time that I took the plunge into understanding inference. It was the entry point into this year's work. I was drawing a model of inference on a loop, allowing multiple sets to do inference. Actually, it's a really good, shall we call it, it's a really interesting systemic design or idea that I explored. It was later referred to as the jazz band in OpenLight. Why don't you check out the strange of AGI and see how much is available there? If not, I'll have to recall and explain everything myself."]},"id":"1M7J7W9674MT0AG","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
