{"body":{"text":["I'm not sure the loop is a primitive. It's just an idea I haven't really given much effort for. I've been focused more on the OpenLite part of programs, interfaces, and these things. The integration with the external world is not so explored yet. But really, there is in and there is out. That's it. It's I/O. Now, if you try to commit something, the only thing about the loop is that I'm considering, is why I wanted to bring it up as well, is because they might write the same buffer, and then it was a really interesting way that it's not just, dumb I/O. It's a bit more than that. So if a few modules run on a cycle and they all write to the same output node, then that output node will reject that and it will become visible in its own state, right? In its own kind of holon history or whatever. And then the others would see that and they'd be able to communicate between each other. They're able to have bodies, identity, express intent, so on. Not that I know that there is a big value in that or not. It was just an idea I had then, but it's still an interesting requirement, right? And the nature of the system we're doing is it's to be open-ended for anything you need to do. That's the whole point. So that's why I'm considering what are the real primitives here and what do they look like."]},"id":"1M7J7W9674MT0NM","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
