{"body":{"text":["When you say your buffer keeps only now, are you giving enough credence to what the buffer really is? The buffer is something that has... I think we should get into the buffer a little bit. This is important before we begin to work further on the engine, which is the next task of settling here. The buffers, it has a way of mapping, right, to data as well, because the buffer has its inherent conversion. So why don't you pull up everything regarding the buffer? I think a simple word search expression against the engine, correlating that, reading all of the utterances and exchanges around that, collecting that together. That's important. Because there is a certain first class thing about buffers, we need to consider that as well. Is buffer really thing here, or really defining a store and a buffer? Or is it so that what the buffer requires is something wider that can be mapped to the larger perspective of this discussion? An integration is a program. That's the traditional view of OpenLight. But again, doesn't make the interface a program. I don't know if I'm ready to stand by that. Maybe there are integrations without history. There are integrations with. The strata may have, some things may have. What does that mean? If it doesn't have, can it interface? If not, is integration responsible then of handling its history itself? There's lots of interesting perspectives here, and I still do not feel that this conversation is truly holding space for it."]},"id":"1M7J7W9674MT2MB","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
