{"body":{"text":["Well, if something doesn't have history, then you can't rely on it, can you? You can't, it's no longer pure. You can't rely on the same result. If there is no means of identity of its content. I think that's it, perhaps not history. You may know then if it changed. It's another thing if you want to save it, and then it's really up to the integration. If the integration wants to implement its own store for caching, etc., storing history of something that doesn't have it, fine. Let's say FTP. We have an FTP reader. Perhaps what it has to do then, if FTP doesn't have history, is to cache everything it reads. And so it then builds history up of it. So we know that FTP and Git and HTTP and the file system all represent the same thing, directories and files. So maybe directories and files are something that is a common type that these show. That's another complication that I can't see straight around. But the most important questions here is, we currently have something we refer to as integrations. There are currently both sources and the interface. Are they programs? Well, we need to see clearly that a program that is run in a runtime, a program that is run through the engine is what we refer to as a program. It may be a program in the traditional sense that is run outside of the engine in whatever hosts it, that's fine. But again, a program is of course always runnable outside of it. No, that's not true. Programs may completely rely on being able to work with the field. But a program such as a store does not rely on being, well, it kind of does through its connection that is then the wire. And that's true. All programs that are run and want to consume things in the engine, they also consume the wire. So they are programs that can be run outside of a runtime, but they don't really serve much of a purpose without the engine, though they definitely could be used with something else that provides similar capabilities, of course. So maybe programs are programs, regardless if managed by the engine or not. I don't know. In my mind, I've already said I feel like I have limited bandwidth, but I can't see the difference between an interface and a kind of store or integration or something like that. Well, it is, as you've said, that may be a difference in terms of the interface reading the engine but then anyway allowing edits. But when those edits are done, that's really then a commit. That commit oh yeah, okay, that's WYSWIG, sure. I don't know, I don't have the brains today."]},"id":"1M7J7W9674MT3VC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
