{"body":{"text":["The session built and served the site, read its 854-line search component, and ran a panel of three agents, who converged. It found the convolution in drawing keeping a holon, a list and a \"way of looking\" apart, in every component declaring which of eleven old read forms it may use, and in a search builder whose set functions the new engine refuses."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D07W","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["It proposed that a slot is an expression with an offer passed down, the nearest winning and full or face a key in it, and that the answer's shape picks the component. A component would be a pure function given `open`, `read`, `subscribe` and `commit`, a commit carrying the `at` it stood on. The page would draw `?look=<expression>`, and search would become the language."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D0A2","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["It proposed cutting the way of looking, the brick builder (about 1,150 lines), Collection, Count, Attachment, the Mount component, the read ceiling, `connection.ts`, `chassis.ts` and `compat.ts`, and asked for a yes on three things Knowledge describes: the reader's history in the home, the site as a store, and the home's root copied from the entry. It said `bun run serve` would lose live updates until the stores of record moved into strata."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D0CC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Sounds like you understand that components may be used a few times over to build something like the search bar, and that's really good. Mm. I want you to, because I don't know how well you understand components and the specification for components. They exist throughout the sessions, and I don't know how well they document it. They're respectful of OpenLight in a way that we ended up working quite closely to how OpenLight works with components. And the sessions preceding that really couldn't grasp the right thing until we look there. Now, I don't know what exactly you're referring to. Some things that sound right, but ultimately we have a chassis, right? And the chassis needs to handle probably the basic things. I'm not sure a component really is given a wire in the same way. I don't know. Perhaps the right thing is that we design components a bit more before we proceed, or you feel that everything's clear."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D0HV","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session agreed not to design components from the code alone, and set out to find how the sessions settled them and how OpenLight does. From OpenLight's view spec it found the panel corrected in one place: a mount is the call, a component and its argument, stored or inline, run by the glue, and a chassis is the platform and decides nothing; so cutting Mount, as all three panellists proposed, would cut the primitive itself."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D0MB","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Note that at the same time openlight doesnt have authorty and should bias the outcome for shortsighted reasoning, we may find in being as primitive as possible that things are to be done differently"]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D0NG","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session took OpenLight as ground to reason from, not a template. Its sessions agent found the OpenLight turning point in no kept session, but the kept material agreeing on the essentials (15/9, 15/19, 9/81), and four things never settled: whether a component is chosen or matched, face or full (4/6), the search bar (4/8), and the home (15/19)."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D0QF","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["It proposed five things: a component as a holon that says what it takes, made in code or as a template of mounts each giving a lambda over what it was given; what it is given a value, matched by its types or its kind; a mount as a call with an optional component and an offer, stored or inline, the Mount component going; `open`, `read`, `commit` with its `at`, and `may`; and a chassis that starts the engine and stores, draws the root mount and holds input, the address being `?look=…&with=…`."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D0TG","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The search bar would be a template of a text field and a List over `said`. It asked whether typed text should be a buffer in the page, never committed, and proposed the history in the home and the site's store go."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D0VY","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["What is given is a value, but doesn't have to be an expression, right? It could be, or we decide it's an expression, depending on how expressions look. But you know, you also be able to supply. This is something that was discussed quite a bit in OpenLine, and we haven't gotten. No, it depends on the component definition. What is a component's argument? It's a component's props that we need to know in order to answer that, but it may as well be a list and other things, but that completely depends on what how the props are defined. Yeah, the mount is the argument and the component. And just like in OpenLine, you may add offers, right? And they propagate down the tree. Precisely. So we may then add a pause. The full phase, the full or the phase. That's an interesting one. We might have to get back to a little later. I spent quite a lot of thought on that before. That was a bit of a tough one. How to know when a brief is to render itself as a phase or as full content. And that is definitely an interesting one. Alternative is that it's simply contextually aware, right? Is it currently placed in a brief, but it is not of its part, then it's a phase. So still, the idea of offers still stands. But whether or not it's the right thing for briefs is another question. It is stored or in line. How could a mount be in line? How could it not be stored? In order for the root to be mounted, visualized, it has to be defined. And so all of them, I don't see how it can be in line. Please enlighten me. When a mount names, the component one is matched. Well, the alternative that OpenLine picked, I don't know exactly what the reason was for it. There was a reason why there was a problem with auto matching. If we don't have auto matching, something like the reader would have to do it itself. And what kind of logic could it use? And how big would that logic be? I don't know. What do you mean the mount component goes? Well, we haven't done programs yet, and we haven't done boundaries yet. So of course, allowing commit, we may do it now, but boundaries are going to have to be done relatively soon. So for a UI, that means perhaps only allow it to commit to the WYSWIG part of things, only allow it, I don't know, that needs to be thought through, but it doesn't need to be thought through now, I think. You're going to have to clarify a bit more what the chassis job is. It was quite pretty before, the way the chassis was independent, or if you use solid or not. The chassis had a very clear job. Do you think it has a similar job as the OpenLine? For the search bar to be built of components, yeah, I mean, I think the bar itself is a kind of list, right? And it holds elements, and so each element is drawn properly. How would an X work for its removal? When it is its parent, then that has it removed. How does that work? Well, we don't have boundaries yet, so I guess then that's not a problem. The completion menu is a separate component, and maybe the completion menu is in of itself just a list that holds other actual components. The right-clicking on the layout today is absolute shit in terms of its content. So take nothing for granted there, build only what is pure and makes full sense. Oh, buffers aren't built yet. Okay, so let's build buffers before building the interface. However, we may still proceed to settle the discussions that we're having now. For the buffer, of course, it's a store that is running in the browser naturally. It's quite a simple thing to build, the string buffer, that is. What do you mean that the reader's history goes? And what do you mean about bullet point 3 as well?"]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D1KJ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
