{"body":{"text":["I can't argue for the cost, honestly. ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D7YG"}," I'm going to raise an idea now that's going to need to be persisted. This session now has various topics that's going to need to be honored appropriately in the field. Through whatever means we're going to need to settle. I know there is something called threads, etc., but graph-wise, I think we're quite poor so far. I'm thinking that as a consumer of the engine, and the engine really being a relay across the joins of multiple stores, there is something that hasn't been fully honored yet. ",{"$holds":true,"$ref":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D822"}," So that goes with commits just as well it goes over the field. So as you squash a section together, you may squash its commits as well. That means that you have a surface that is quicker to index and read, but you also have something that is lesser of size. And that which is deeper of size may be stored still, but is stored in a way where it is detachable. So the way that we treat our object model, the way we treat our stores, our storages, is in a way where, right, you can pick and leave. So as I am querying a store into my own store, I may choose at what degree I do that. Now what you have at you is still whole, right? I can wipe the other one. I only then have what I have. Should I get access again? Right, that kind of perspective is really interesting, because that's the fact that the field is big and costly is true, but that completely resolves it. And that is ultimately what is ordered all the way up to the interface and the human interaction as well. So you look upon the field, you see available depths of fidelity, then you may load those on demand as to not be forced to have everything immediately. But going closer to the current topic, a link being two ways interesting. Definitely is, because if a function is two-way and you put it in an expression and you connect multiple two-way functions together, then you're building a larger two-way function. And really what we're defining here is that we have functions that can come together through various orchestrations as a flow, and sometimes things can flow one way and it can also flow the other. Now that definition of functions piping into each other is the definition of the interface, right? At a root, I take a certain expression of the field. Now I hold that channel. And so I may then, through the use of a component, have more slots be distributed, right? Manually, I may mount specific things. I may relay further. And it goes on and goes on. The kind of link we're talking about is that channel. I don't see what has to be complicated about this, really."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D8EY","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session set down the author's grain: an expression says the grain at which it looks, for commits as for holons; a squashed section squashes its commits; the finer grain is kept detachably; one taking a store chooses the depth and holds what it took whole, so nobody holds the whole field at full depth. It likened this to Git's partial clones and map tiles, took a brief's face or full as grain, and named the one rule this needs, that a link into a grain not held is 'deeper, not here', never dead. It conceded the convolution had been its own and agreed the link is the channel: held at the root, carried forward, carried back where every function has a codec, codecs composing, a component distributing it into slots. It listed what was decided and what open and offered to record the session as the nineteenth, with a thread for each open question linked to what it depends on."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D8M5","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["We need not to persist yet, for we've just made a big leap. Then let's take it further and actually see what it looks like. Not the grain thing, first the function composition. So expressions were previously text strings, but they now become real. In the field made of holons. Any function may be built on more functions, just as components may be built on templates. Ultimately, down there, functions can be native or can be provided by your own code in whatever runtime is made available. That part, though, is about runtimes, I don't know yet. Anyway, if I use the function in my mount, and I then want to reference to it, well, the function may be defined particularly for that purpose, or it may be a wider function. That function does not necessarily have, well, it depends. The function usually has an input, but it's possible that here an expression really just takes something in the field and builds upon it. Now the question is if I want to reuse. Reusing is the same thing as patching into its output, really. It's just reusing it and attaching it in your expression. You ready to draw an example up? Keeping it creative for the means of expressiveness with simplicity, because I don't want to be overloaded."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D8VH","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session drew a small patch: `briefs-of`, built from the native `parts` and `only briefs` with one socket left open; `knowledge-briefs` plugging `@Knowledge` into it; a list mounted on its output and `count` patched into the same output for a badge. It concluded that a function is an expression with a socket left open, that reuse is patching into an output with nothing copied, and that at the bottom a box has no inside, only code."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D8Y0","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["And I get it, you're back to drawing these things I don't understand at all. Crazy. Absolutely crazy. Draw the example of the search bar and the store and things then perhaps instead. Or other examples. The code block first, just a big overhead to follow your instructions, only to have to patch things up on, I mean, fucking hell."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D904","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session conceded the drawing was not clear and laid out the search bar plainly: a `location` store giving `location/q`, a native `parse` with `back = print`, a use `the-query`, and a `reader` component with mounts `bar` (search) and `place` (naming no component, found by what it gives), the page mount given `the-query`. It walked loading, typing `| text`, which runs back through `print` into `location/q` and moves the URL, and pressing back."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D92F","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Okay, so I'm gonna speak out loud a little bit because I don't know, the visualizations that you do, they still don't resonate with me. First of all, let's allow ourselves to, because it's very flat when we write like this, so we could allow nesting where it's, now I see there's only one holds here. But let's say that that could rest because there is still a holarchy, right? Well, it also depends. The mounting of the page and given might be holds as well, right? But further than that, it still doesn't truly resonate with me. Well, essentially what you're doing is that you're inventing function and use. Is that necessary? Why? The point of the use then is to take a function and plug something into its input. Yeah, that makes sense. The interesting thing here is that you're forgiven, then you're using the address of the field itself. Which is a little bit of a strange thing. Because a component can be used in various mounts. So that's kind of then a special resolution kind of thing. The alternative would be to reuse the actual the query use block. The problem with that is when if you want to exchange it, then you have to exchange at all locations. What does this mean for other expressions that we have? I don't know, functional use to me just, maybe it's because I think it's so low fidelity that it's cumbersome, but that's ultimately what we're doing. Though we still allow the syntax for writing expressions, in TypeScript and/or as a string. Perhaps you can draw a few cases of expressions as well, where you show the expression as a string one-liner and maybe you visualize it as a node graph and then you show the code for it, such as the HCL that we're using here, but allowing nesting where nesting is, as I've said. How does the bar work here? That's also something I wanted to see, so that's a more complex example of where the bar is actually laid out as data, right? And so it's able to break apart the expression as well into its constituents. And there may be some other practical case of using components and actually, you know, having expressions depend on previous expressions in various ways. And for the back thing, I don't know, is that really the best way of doing it? That the function has a reverse? Because the alternative is that a function has to implement both ways. But then it's a bit problematic because how do you know which way is, what's its polarity, right? But because yeah, sometimes you just want to use it the other way, even though it's coming from holarchically from top to bottom. It's not always the same polarity you want to use it with. It could be the reverse. So then maybe functions can come in pairs if they both name each other in their reverse. Here the function parse is also a bit small. I would expect it to have some other fields, right? I mean, it's a native function, for instance. It doesn't have any code, etc. But I'm still a bit skeptical here. I understand that you use because you need to actually mount something to its argument. And that's what an expression is. You're plugging in various functions. Yeah, maybe there's no way around it, but I still don't like it."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D9NT","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session proposed dropping use and mount for glass's own pair, a type and its instances: a call an instance of its function, a mount an instance of its component, written with nesting so held parts and namespace are one. It drew three expressions as one-liner, flow and nested HCL, an expression built on another( `recent-briefs` patched into `recent` ),and the search bar laid out as data, `expression` inside `reader` found by looking upward like a parameter, a chip's removal running back through `steps` / `join` and `parse` / `print` into `location/q`. It took up the author's pairs over a single `back`: `parse` and `print` each a whole function with `takes`, `gives`, `native` and `reverse`, no direction privileged, the naming required to be mutual or refused, the laws allowed to be asymmetric."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D9T7","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Right. Before we had mounts to be instances of components in OpenLight, but there was a reason we had to switch it around. So we already went through this and decided to not do it. So you need to find out why."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0D9VQ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session searched OpenLight's history and found the ruling in its surface proposal: a mount as an instance of the component was replaced, in the same cut as props-as-key-value gave way to argument as selection, by a mount as an instance of `view/mount`. An instance's type says what its body is, and once a component takes a list of types matched by fit it describes no body; and instance may be claimed by anyone and several at once, while a call must name one callee, so the callee became a single strict field, the steward's slip into' `view/mount` -as-multi-typing' caught in the log. It withdrew its proposal as props-as-key-value through the side door: a call is its own type naming one callee and giving it a list, perhaps one `call` type for functions and components, nesting kept only as a way of writing it."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DA0D","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Also reading here, I'm a bit confused by it. So whenever there is something written in this HCL and it doesn't have a key, it's a nested definition. It's held. Because something that is held still has to be at a key, doesn't it? So maybe you can come up with a better HCL. Because I'm a bit confused reading it still. I actually don't get this example, like size, filter, type. I'm not able to translate it in my head, unfortunately. So page is an instance of reader. That part of HCL makes sense, that the first thing you write is that it's an instance of a type. But then parse just sits inside of it. That's confusing to me. So now I think you're going to want to explain to me again. Oh, I forgot. Components don't take named properties. The definition of component takes what? A list. Then I'd still see in the reader example, it should be given or its argument takes. Right, takes expression. So I think maybe you're doing a bit of shorthand, and it can't be shorthand here for me. It has to be real. That's a lot of text to understand why OpenLight, but please speak to me more simply. I don't understand why it can't be an instance of. There has to be a separate type."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DA88","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session said simply that a type says what keys a holon's body has, a component says only what it takes, every use has the same keys, what it calls and what it gives it, and that shape is the type `call`; a holon may have several types but a call calls exactly one thing, so `calls` is a key. It wrote the HCL without shorthand by three rules (first word the type then the name; every line `key = value`; a block value held at that key, `ref(x)` pointing elsewhere), and rewrote `log(\"main\") | take(3)` and the reader and page this way, the reader taking a list with one `inlet \"query\"` of type expression and its layout calling `search` and an unnamed call with `ref(query)`."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DAC6","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Why do we have inlets here? Is that necessary? And having something be calls and then has calls as it's a bit hard to read. It should probably something else. It always takes what? So I see no function definition here. Could it be called use and have an argument fn instead? Maybe it's more different and easier to read. And I see no use of holds here, so I guess that's if you see a hold on is defined like that, then it is actually using hold."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DAF9","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session said inlets need no type of their own: each entry in `takes` can be a held, empty placeholder of the type it takes, named only for pointing, like a blank on a form. It agreed to `use` with `fn` and `argument`, so a component is a function whose result is drawn, both defined with `takes` and `body`. It agreed that a block written at a key is held( `$holds`) and `ref(x)` a plain reference, and showed `take`, `first-three`, `reader` and `page` written that way, with one unnamed use in `reader` having no `fn`, found by what it is given."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DAJP","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Interestingly here, a use block in first three doesn't even have a name, which is all right. Names are not mandatory. And given that it is held by, I think that's fine. Because then preferably we always just want to see it in line. When it comes to the human's point of view in an interface. Here we are defining a list with a name, and then we're using a reference to that name. Does that mean that the list items is another hold on that is held? That would make sense. What's the point of expression now? The type expression. Oh, did you drop amount in favor of use for interfaces as well? What about offers and these things? Is that something we want to build into the same primitive?"]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DAQ8","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session said `list \"items\" {}` is a holon held by `first-three` at `takes`, found by name among what it holds, so holding is the namespace; names stay optional. It folded the type `expression` into `use`, since an expression is a tree of uses, with one rule: an input that takes a `use` gets the use itself, anything else gets what the use gives. Mount is gone, a use whose `fn` is a component. Of OpenLight's mount, `offer` comes onto every use, filling optional inputs of its type anywhere beneath, while draw waits for a second surface and reach waits for boundaries."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DATJ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["In your example, I don't see how optional can be parts of the body of density, rather optional keys, optional field values is something. However, in here it's in a list because that's how it is. It is not key value naturally. So I don't know how we define optionals. That probably we need to have specific syntax for providing optionals. I'm fine with the dropping mount until we feel the need of it again. I don't quite get the case of where you get the use or you get the value of a use. So we know the reader takes a query. Sorry, no. It takes any use. We, however, name it query. So this is a bit strange to me. Takes here, we're defining types kind of. But when we, can you please give me this new way of thinking? Let's see here. The reader, no, I'll go to the page first. Page is a use. Of a component. So a root mount for your kind of chassis, it can only take a use that references a component, right? You can't just reference a function. So the definition of use is that it takes both functions and components. That's maybe a bit chaotic, but we need to reason around that. So looking at the reader, takes an expression, it has a body. Which takes another component list, provides a list of the use of search referencing the query, and then just a raw use without a function. What is that? A raw use without a function. Okay. Well, we do know that in this case, by defining the use, we get the very expression it is. Okay. But we still don't see how the search component's built. How would it take the use and break it down into its pills, so to say? And can that be done with layout as data? Because if it can't, then we have a problem with layout as data and it's not mature enough."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DB61","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session conceded that `optional = true` in the density holon's body was wrong: being optional is a fact of the entry, so it goes on the link as `$optional`, written `optional direction \"direction\" {}`. It said `takes` is a parameter list, a type to fit and a name as handle. A use may call a function or a component, and who draws it decides the difference, so the root needs no restriction; it dropped the raw use with no `fn` as a muddle, for a small code component `answer`. It laid out search's pills as data, `each(steps(query), pill)` in a row beside `expression-input`, with × flowing back through `each`, `steps` ⇄ `chain` and `parse` ⇄ `print` into `location/q`, and judged layout as data able, given `each`, function pairs, items knowing their place, and code at the leaves; search would be the first build to prove it."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DBAQ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Well, looks like we're inventing what would be favored by Lisp. Now, the use of, and that's, I don't know, I mean, we are making data in its right format per the principles of hold-ons and everything, but reading it's a bit of a mess. Um, And use and function and component, I guess they're all just hold-ons with their own type, but they are kind of reserved, I think, for a syntactically different presentation, or in a UI a different presentation. But it's extensible in that way. I mean, you could add any other hold-on that has a specific UI presentation, and that could be done, I guess. So it's open-ended still, but still a very primal thing. So how, show me now how nicely you could write this instead of being honest with its holonic definition."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DBFA","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session agreed it is Lisp's insight that code is data, the difference being that every part here is a holon. It wrote the same definitions in a readable function-call spelling, `first-three(items: list) = take(items, 3)`, with a pipe form for search, and a table mapping each written form to its holons( `name(params) = body`, `direction?: direction`, `f(x, y)` a held `use`, `= native` ). Pairs as `parse ⇄ print` and offers as `offering`. It said the text is one more face over the same holons, read and printed by a pair, and any type may have its own spelling, so the set stays open."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DBJJ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Please use syntax for your code blocks so they're easy to read. Here, though, you're allowing them to be drawn as components, sorry, as functions, like classic syntactical functions. Yet, I'm not sure how that translates. Is list, each, steps, expression, input, all functions or components that take that has the field takes. Oh, you have a map table here. Let's see. Okay, so you're doing a first class thing for takes, all right. Perhaps we shouldn't call it takes then, or we should call it params or something. Okay, so and you don't think that Lisp makes more sense here?"]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DBP2","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session agreed `params` is a better name than `takes`, and tabled which names are functions and which components, all with `params`, most native. It argued Lisp fits definitions better because its text is the tree: each parenthesis one held use, no precedence or pipes, so reading and printing are exact; it wrote the definitions in Lisp, with `:reverse print` and `:offer`, and kept pipes for typing queries in the bar."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DBRC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I don't know, I feel this session is getting quite lengthy and out on a limb here. I don't know, I think Lisp is hard for people to read. People are not used to it. But the whole, I don't know, and the idea here of coming up with the better syntax that is easier to read. It is necessary, for sometimes we are in text, we aren't using a UI to look upon things. But, and the way of using the honest HCL for hold-ons, it is helpful, it is. Question is if we want to build an actual primitive for functions. So what is a function really? Well, maybe both a component and a function are both functions, if we make functions a first-class thing in the field. But maybe that's unnecessary. Maybe that's wrong. I don't know. So it's quite a long session here. We're going out into all kinds of things, and I'm not sure how we're going to collect it here."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DBY2","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session agreed the session had gone wide and proposed gathering. It said a function is a primitive only if expressions become holons, and judged that direction right but not needed to move the interface onto the new engine: components with `params`, mounts as uses and expressions still as text would carry it. It listed what was settled, what leaned (expressions as holons, links as two-way channels, `offer` on every use, a readable spelling but not Lisp) and what was open, and proposed recording the session as the nineteenth with each open matter as its own thread."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DC18","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["No, I don't think that we should go ahead and update the interface per the old paradigm. We are talking about something that is shifting the whole field. So no, I disagree. Let's not drop it and do other things first. This is major. Now, the first thing that I stated is a component and a function just normal holons like everything else? Or is it a particular first-class syntactical thing in the field of holons? The function. That a holon can be a function, right? That a holon actually takes arguments. Then that's different, you see? And then it doesn't matter if it's a component or something else, it is a holon that takes arguments. Now, such a function is still a holon. Still, it takes code, sure, if it needs to, or it continues with more function. But it's still agnostic, it's still up to the chassis to resolve it, because we still create components, but it is still a function as a native thing, you see? I don't know if that's the right thing to do. We keep everything just as holons in the open-ended way it is. For a UI, it's never really a problem, but it's here when it comes to text that everything gets a bit harder to reason about."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DC8M","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session agreed not to drop it, and proposed making taking arguments a primitive of the holon beside holding and referring: `params` as held placeholders and `use` (here `apply` and `argument`) applying one holon to others, so any holon may take arguments, a brief included (\"Welcome, ‹who›\"). The types `component` and `function` would remain only to say who carries them out. It grounded this in the lambda calculus, abstraction and application, and said applying would then have one spelling everywhere, `name(args)`."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DCBB","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Okay, this is a big pivot point. I've been thinking about holonic computing for more than half a year now, and this was never, never in my mind until today. It's a big shift. It's interesting, but what's really the consequences of this? Anything can become a function. Do you want that? Do you want a brief to be able to become a function? I don't know. Suggest you call up a big panel on this. We really want to ground in the purpose of what we're doing and the evolution of our project. I don't know quite how to ground myself in this yet. Just the point to it is that it's always pure. What does this mean to other things, such as programs and so on? I don't know. It's a very open-ended thing, and it might be undesirable. Unless it's up to the archetype whether or not it can be a function or not. I don't know."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DCGY","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session convened a panel of eight on four options: every holon may take arguments (A), a type decides (B), function and component as types the engine knows (C), or nothing changes (D). The panel found much of the leap already in glass: expressions already kept as data( `{call, with}` ),68 function holons with `takes`, `gives` and `native`, mount already described as a call, and `statistic` waiting for functions as holons. The session conceded it had pushed A without rereading Knowledge, and all eight rejected it, since a holon must be complete, readable alone and the same everywhere. Five chose B, and the two who chose C, the ontologist and the red team, agreed with it in practice: a type's mould declares that its instances take arguments, purity is the gate, programs are started never applied, briefs and the record never declare it. What converged was one shape for function and component, mount becoming use, the evaluator reading the mould instead of `name === \"function\"` at `evaluate.ts:73`, uses kept inline unless named or shared, and `statistic` as a use; the deciding question was whether a holon may have holes."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DCQ0","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Yeah, but is it really about taking arguments as much as it is templating, right? Taking an argument for the sake of templating. There is nothing actually run, right? That is still up to the type you declare and whoever handles it. So there may be a function that has a defined, you know, type on it or whatever. Native means that the engine runs it. And maybe there's another, type of expression, or it can take different kinds of, or if it takes code, then depending on supported format, it's able to run it and all kinds of things. But it's just the templating, being able to pass things through. Maybe I'm hallucinating. Maybe that's not what it's about."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DCV4","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session agreed that applying is templating: it fills declared holes with links and nothing runs; what a filled holon means belongs to its type's handler, the engine, a chassis, a runtime, or no one for a brief. It argued this removes purity as a gate and keeps a holon complete, since the template is one holon with its holes showing and each application is its own use. The remaining cost is real: a filled result is derived, not citable or indexed unless committed. It leaned back toward generic templating, with a type's mould able to refuse holes where stability matters."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DCYF","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I dont know, it is a clear drawn lien then between definitions and templated defenitions. What is the win in this and what is th eloss. I suggest another panel"]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DCZJ","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session convened seven on wins, losses, the record, engine and index, people and interface, precedents, and the line itself, over generic templating, templating gated by type, or nothing new. Every voice that chose chose gated by type: gated can widen later but generic cannot narrow; generic fills reach deep into words and cost copies; filled content looks present but cannot be found or cited; MediaWiki, Helm and Figma returned to declared holes. The voice on the line found the question dissolves: the line is held against derived, already drawn in a type's mould, and templating already exists as function, component, mount, statistic and type itself, so no new primitive is needed. The win needed now is one kind of application in place of call, mount and statistic. Rules any way: holes declared and typed, uses live and pinnable with a wider `at`, templates unable to silently break uses, filled results derived unless committed as a stamp, and an edit landing where the text came from."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DD4Z","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["I need you to give me your updated take, under the rule. Having read the knowledge holon today, brief me on what has changed"]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DD5V","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session briefed that glass already had the parts and the day found how they are one: no new primitive, holes in every holon rightly turned down, and one kind of application in place of call, mount and statistic, with holes opened by a type's mould, function and component first, and the evaluator reading the mould. The line between held and derived stays where Knowledge draws it. It answered two of Knowledge's open questions (a component picked once and written into the application; declared once and realized per surface), gave new rules (live applications pinnable, types unable to silently break applications, edits landing at their source, writing back through mutual pairs that propose a change), said expressions stay one nested body of data, and put the buffer store first, then one application."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DDA4","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["An application is live by default and may pin its template's version. That does make sense, but it's really just at a certain specific commit anyway, which is something I guess you can already do. So what's the problem? A type can't silently break its applications by dropping or retyping a holiday field. That I don't understand. An edit lands where the text came from. I don't understand that either. Final bullet looks to be the pair. That is a good addition. Through the field reverse. I don't think we have takes though. I'm not sure takes is the best name. Perhaps input is the most pure of them. Arguments is not bad either, or args. Expressions stay one nested body of data. That I do not know what it means. I guess you mean that it's simply namespaced under where it's consumed, unless it's reused and it gets its own. Not that you're saying that it is to be part of the body. It is simply namespaced. How a function is found by the types it takes and gives. I don't know what you mean by that. What keeps a whole on the same across commits? Don't know what you mean by that. Whether a relation has direction. Or why are you talking about that? Why is that a relevant discussion? Yeah, the grain and detachable depth is to be continued. But it's not a blocker for us resolving this. I think we should settle our function and expression discussion here so that we can proceed and update the engine and then the interface, get onto main, and then we can do other things. The integrative side between stores, I don't know what you mean about that. Do you mean to say that there is nothing to edit from here? Because still, I don't get it. We still need to invent some new holons, don't we, for expressions? What about the whole thing about being able to patch, etc.? That is not clear to me."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DDNS","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
