{"body":{"text":["The session said there is something to build and its last brief undersold it: every call becomes a holon of a new type, `use`, with `fn`, `args` and `offer`, held by what consumes it, as the author read it, namespaced there rather than part of its body, and named when reused, so a pill can be pointed at and patching is pointing at a use's output. It withdrew the panel's advice to keep uses inline as JSON, as two forms of one thing, judging the round trip per layer small. It set out the work: in the engine, the function's inputs as held typed placeholders with `$optional` on the link, built functions with a held `use` body, lambdas as small held functions, `reverse` pairs, the evaluator reading the mould, the text language as the first pair, judging args, and converting the 13 field functions, statistics, mounts and the Reader template; in the interface, components declaring inputs in their holons, mount becoming use, and the search bar's pills through `steps` ⇄ `chain`. Of the author's names it took `input` for the definition and `args` for the use. It conceded pinning is no new problem and dropped it, explained the breaking-change rule as a later judging rule, dropped \"an edit lands where the text came from\" as following by itself, set aside finding a function by its types as able to wait, and dropped sameness across commits, direction and the integrative side as not belonging here, with grain to continue later. The order: engine, then interface, then main."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DDZD","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Finding a function by its type, naturally you should be able to have the functions to ask that. You should be able to ask any holon in any way about the body, right? The fields, its references. Of course, you should be able to ask any such question. So I don't see anything special with function. Right, good. So, begin by persisting this session. I don't recall if that's been done before. Of course, call a few sub-agents, both on your summaries of your responses and on my prompt, so that there is a triple checking that everything's been persisted correctly, nothing's lost or distorted. And then it sounds like you've got quite a lot of work to do throughout everything. And I guess you would make an appropriate plan for that. Because as I read this, it looks like all kinds of things are going to need implementation. There is new kind of shipped holons that come with things, but there is new native functions probably. I don't recall anymore, but there's obviously work all over the place, and then there is updating of the interface. So I trust that you will do that appropriately. Good luck."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DE65","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session kept itself in the store: the author's words as they came, its answers by their essence, the paths it noted; it gave Knowledge what was settled of functions and expressions, and raised the thread Grain and detachable depth. Blank readers checked the record against the transcript and Knowledge against the author's words. It then planned the work, in the engine first and then the interface."]},"id":"1M7R4Z0KHV0DE8D","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["On those three questions, use common sense"]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A8008","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session settled by common sense the three questions it had put once the engine read uses, the buffer stood and the page's first two stages drew on the new engine: a component's web code lives in the web chassis, mapped by the component's id, the holon declaring and each surface realizing; the reader and search take the query's text, search parsing it itself so the way back ends in print; brief stays as code, the other views fall back to the plain value view to return as data if missed, face and full wait, and the home goes, history being the browser's. It began stage 3, the search bar made of uses rather than code: steps and chain as a pair, each with a way back item by item, the pill component, and what the page needs shipped in glass, as the test of whether a component can be built from data."]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A8055","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Start this project's dev server from. claude/launch.json."]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A805F","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session found another serve, likely the stage 3 build checking its work, holding the site's lock and port, attached the preview to it, and once stage 3 was committed started the glass configuration from. claude/launch.json on port 4330, saying the app's preview pane cannot run the new page, whose workers start inside a worker, so it is to be opened in Chrome. It reported the test of layout as data as mixed: the arrangement of reader and search is data shipped in glass, while the leaves, the ways back through each, steps and chain, and dropping an item from a list are still code. It named three rough edges, ids in the address after a pill is removed, the last pill unremovable, and components marked named standing in for finding, and a clash of names, components kept capitalised since a lowercase search would meet the native search, against the author's lean to lowercase names, to settle when the stores move. Stage 4 began: a component found by type and the pick written into the use, brief brought back, declarations moved into holons."]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A80BC","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Next, please use a professional approach to reviewing the actual application in terms of user experience and design. There's definitely some things that don't feel right with clicking around, loading, etc. The menu you get on right click makes no sense at all. The spacing is irregular between presented cards in a list. It also seems to have its own padding on the left and right, which is horrendous. I mean, and that doesn't make any sense at all. I guess what would be good with the right click is to actually see some information maybe about, say, the path, which is in that place, and also to because I'm asking for the path as well, because I couldn't even find the layout of the website where it is. There's a start, but then there's an entry, and I press it, and then I can't even get any further. And also, like, I don't know what component that is, but it says, you know, is an instance of, holds, entry. I guess that's the kind of table or general kind of hold on. Just look at it, honestly. It's a tremendously low-balling design. So given your capability and seriously considering what's appropriate, I think you ought to be able to actually just sit down and actually use the application and reason around it and elevate it so it's actually a good experience, because right now it really isn't. Yeah, it's a proof of concept maybe that rendering works, but it's not really usable beyond that. I think you shouldn't be able to press any card and it takes you out of the reader. And now that there is a kind of hierarchical relationship to things, if I press something and that is navigating down into hierarchy, I think the reader should probably have a breadcrumb, right? I know the briefs have it anyway, but maybe the briefs shouldn't have the breadcrumb anymore, since the reader is anyway allowing for hierarchical navigation. The point about when right-clicking something in the interface is to be able to see that particular mount, right? Just to be able to get that type of information is probably helpful. There's also lots of flashing as you move around, which is, of course, not nice. And we definitely want to use full width. I don't get why we wouldn't want to use full width. And the reader's bar, I don't know, having such pills on an actual background bar. Yeah, I guess that's fine. But then if everything's full width, then I don't know what that makes of the design. And the bar needn't have a background then, because it already has a background. So then things can just pass behind it. But those things are just common design sense. So why don't you take it as far as you can, naturally not complexing things as a result of it, but naturally there's lots of improvement that you can do."]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A80VR","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session used the page itself in a real browser, through start, entry and right-click, and found what the author described and more: a narrow column with cards inset again and uneven gaps, a boxed bar showing ids, fields read like a debug dump, dead ends with nothing showing where one is, a right-click menu saying nothing of the place, flashing, and an empty start. It wrote the review as a design brief for the new interface, which takes over/ when the old page goes, and after stage 4 landed, components found by type and a brief drawn as a brief, built the pass: full width with one margin and one spacing scale, prose alone keeping a reading measure; the bar sticky with no box, its input continuing the pills; every card moving within the reader without flashing; a breadcrumb walking up what holds the holon shown; right-click as an inspector of that place, the component drawing it, what it was given, the uses down to it and the holon itself, leading to the reader's own layout; a designed record ending in what refers to it; and the stores to start from. Before committing it sent back the query shown twice and an inspector framing the wrong card, and it named two trade-offs, the answer updating on Enter rather than as one types, and brief sections still taking about 0.4 s."]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A8134","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["All right, I'm reviewing it. Obviously, some things have gotten better, but some things I'd like to resolve. I'd like to take inspiration from the rules surface a little bit. First off, I expect more coherent typography sizes. So we've got the smallest one is the body text, and it's used in the cards and in general text. You can see that the title in the card is a notch bigger, but then the typography in the top bar is even bigger. Like in the place in which you type, it's even bigger, which is just really weird. I think the surfaces that are generated aren't really that expressive, and we certainly expect the field to be very expressive through the components naturally. It's also so that I think that the layout of the lists are also, it certainly is gonna get a bit of upgrading. So we don't utilize the full width, that is obvious. Okay, yeah, sure, if I get wide enough, I see that it actually wraps, but then there's still lots of space to the left, which doesn't quite make sense to me. There's also design language that uses lines to cut, which doesn't resonate with me. So I think either we use Glass's approach of letting things fade, and so then the top bar can really just be quite naked, and then content can just fade before it. But then it would have to fade at the bottom as well. The alternative now may be that the top bar has its own background surface and is naturally on top, so things just slide under it, and you can actually even see the content slightly above the kind of floating bar before it hits the edge of the viewport. So I don't know currently what makes a card, because the cards all over the place is quite they have a title and then they have something below, but if I look upon something, such as a lexicon, there's definitely more to know. Though I guess since it just contains lots of children, there isn't that much more than the count in it, right? But a lexeme, it obviously has content, it has a text field. And still though, I question the lexicon. I don't get the point of it. I thought this is something that is a result of a function, because otherwise you have two sources of truth and it doesn't make sense to me. I'll try to look at another holon. So I pressed on the author's what relates to this, what refers to this. But the typing experience doesn't give me any kind of intelligence or completion. I thought that I would group by or something like that, which I couldn't. Then if I press back, it takes me into the, pops into the next one, which is kind of cool, but, and here it's the author and then in, and then I see that the values in this kind of table that is rendered now has bigger typography, which is weird as well, so that's incoherent. We'll take a look at knowledge. Knowledge's renderings now different. The surface for a brief is also not enough. I mean, the first paragraph should be in there. And the headings definitely should be bigger. In briefs, that is only, right, for prose. Then when it comes to the list, so it's interesting. So when I look at main, it has various sections, right? Contents, dependencies, and shape. I wonder what it is that makes that. The right click is interesting now. I appreciate that. It's more helpful. Onto the list. My point is that I think the list should actually be able to have some toggle modes, but I think it should respect the estate of its children. So obviously it could be a grid of 3 columns when in fact it's just 1, and then there's lots of space for the contents part, but I guess it's because it is a table that it looks like this, and that's understandable, but it doesn't quite work out. Interesting, the type surfaces actually has a paragraph in it. So there the surface does give you more. There's all of these, I'm looking at types now. I see lots of types, that confuses me. Like I see list, item, quote, code, table, strong. Oh, is that for prose? Because then I'd kind of... No, should they all be kept so flat? And what is main? Main is quite a chaotic store anyway. Then there is glass. And glass, I guess, is the necessities required for... But then what is main's purpose? Why has it got so many things? I understand that in some cases the table look is nice, but I guess when it has too much content, that's when a row doesn't quite work out to me, because then you're wasting lots of estate. That's the feedback I have for now."]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A81YS","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session looked at the rule's surface the author named and took from it a naked bar with content fading at top and bottom, the first of the two ways the author offered, body text as the base with big titles only for headings, and cards that open with what the thing is. It built the second pass: one type scale, no lines, cards saying what they hold (a brief's first paragraph, a type's says, a lexeme's word, a whole's counts), lists filling the width by their children with a cards, rows and table toggle remembered per place, records giving lists and long content sections of their own, briefs opening with a lede and bigger prose headings, and completion in the bar, functions after | and holons by name after@, with a native group(list, by) since the author reached for grouping. Answering from the field, it agreed the stored lexicon, 4,486 lexemes beside the 7,141 words lexicon() derives, is two sources of truth already apart and a leftover against Knowledge's word that text is never stored, and left letting it go to the author as a large change; main's sections are its root's fields, main a catch-all until a store can be cut out with its history, and its flat types, prose's among them and some twice, would read better grouped, best done when the stores move."]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A8261","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Interesting. This works to me, but I still think there's a little bit of incoherence with sizes. So the table keys are the smallest, and the breadcrumb has the same size, and then some other things has that size. Then the body text in the card is the middle one, and then there is a somewhat bigger that is used in the card's titles. But I still think, in comparison, the top toolbar is too big. The top toolbar to me is more in the register of the smaller texts. Because I don't see the point of it being bigger. You want to save real estate, of course, there. And I don't know, if I look at something like the surface in the rule, and if you look, you need to look at a wide enough desktop. I think it has all the kind of menu things in appropriate sizes, where here I still think it's a bit incoherent. I can also tell you that we're switching between rows and cards. There is a flash of the outlines as well. for the for reading briefs are still things that the headings actually should grow a bit more. The previous design used something like 0.4 as a scale. I think that works well. And then maybe, of course, ignoring H6, maybe H5 compressing a little bit, or maybe using 1.35 as the ratio. How is completion in the bar made? Is it a separate component, and how is it mounted, etc.? Is it layout as data that's driving it or not? That I'm curious about. Yeah, of course we won't have a stored lexicon. Of course not. Yeah, and I think we won't touch this much more. What next? What we'll do is we'll divide the stores properly, place them properly, and so on, because that's the next thing. We want to work with an appropriately ordered structure. But at least take my feedback first."]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A82H2","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["The session answered that completion is code within the input leaf, ExpressionInput, reading what it offers through the engine as any question, with only the bar's arrangement data, and proposed it might later become a data component taking the text, its options given by a read. It took the feedback: the bar moved into the small register beside table keys and the breadcrumb, every element measured to three sizes and prose headings, headings on a ratio of 1.35 from body with h5 at the notch and 1.25 on phones, and the flash on switching rows and cards traced to a border transition and removed. Its survey of the stores found main four stores tangled, its 51 types belonging to prose, paper, paper-readings and the author, function twice, four types unused and 446 markdown forms unread, and proposed five stores, glass at the repository's root with its types grouped into wholes, knowledge, author, and paper and paper-readings under author, letting go both lexicons, the markdown forms, duplicate types and the site store, through a new cut.ts replaying the old histories, tested by the five equalling the old field. It put eight questions with its own answers: to let the lexicon go by an ordinary commit so history stays true, unless the author counted it an erasure; main's root becoming knowledge's; the old wiki kept as it stood; statistics and the board with the author; git keeping only history, the index rebuilt; permission to glass's store group."]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A82RX","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
{"body":{"text":["Interesting. Well, I it's interesting to place everything like that in the root instead of looking upon the modules as building upon each other. So for instance, all of the interface things could be part of an interface module, right? All of the things that pertain to the engine, which we don't have programs about yet, it's in there. Pros is I guess that we're shipping all kinds of different things, but and having them in their own ownership in the store namespaces, that means that down the line, as it's going to be easy to just load only the namespace that you want and depend only on the namespace you want. It actually has its own structure, so maybe that's fine because we're namespacing inside the store anyway. I don't know, the measures and statistics, they are, I guess they're relating to a particular set of pros hold on, so they should probably be stored with where they are. Sounds fine to create a cut file for now, why not? Well, the lexicon, to me, I never kind of wanted it. And it is anyway going to be in Git history. Lexicon is good because it simulates quite a lot of hold-ons, and so we are, yeah, you can ship it for now, and what we might do later is just compress it. Yeah, but I don't really see a value in it. I don't know if the old wiki has things in it that the knowledge hold-on doesn't, because then we might as well terminate it, and it's still available as commits in history, so if you want to read it you still can. Yeah. Again, what's the definition of wiki? Maybe we don't want to call it the directory knowledge. Maybe we want to call it wiki. But we're definitely going to compress away all kinds of things later, such as the lexicon, etc. Actually, do clear the lexicon. Do clear it. If we want sizable stores, we can always simulate them. So actually read the history of the lexicon. I think that will reduce the size quite a bit. Now there's all kinds of history churn going on that it's good to have it now because we simulate, but we're gonna compress it away later, definitely."]},"id":"1M7Z3667J6A835K","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
