{"body":{"text":["So the visual theme has got the fade up top, similarly to how the surface works. We've, I think we've got a fine method like that. We can continue to try it, but there is a kind of, the fade is, I don't know, I judge maybe just intuitively, maybe 50 pixels long or something, but there is still a very visible edge where the fade meets the background div. And that's wrong. It should be from 0 to 100, fading in a good way. Not sure what algorithm need be, but you're never to feel, and this is part of the design language. So you're never to feel that there is an actual div with the fill, right? We only emulate the fading so that everything just feels like space. Next, the pulse in the search bar is, if it's cleared, then we only have the text input, and the text input doesn't have a border unless it's hoovered. So then the text in it doesn't align to the left, and that's a problem. So we definitely want to solve that either by having a persisted background on the input or do something else about its design. I don't know if we can do a brief outline only, or if that's bad. Probably that's bad is my guess. Also, it has a different height than the pills have, which of course would be a problem, but the font is the same, so I don't see a reason for them not to be the same height. I don't know if we can do something more. But perhaps it is supposed to be filled. The problem is that it's not a pill, right? And so it has a different rotation, so they don't share the space in an equal way. So possibly we make them share the same radius, and it doesn't need to be a pill, but maybe we increase the radius of the cards and of the pills and the input a little bit. I think we should a touch. Then if I'm on the main page, I see a title and then the body text under it, which is a bigger body text, which is fine given that it is, you know, a kind of highlight, but it doesn't completely resonate with me. The margin. So we have the top, then we have margin, then we have the title, glass, then it says one field kept in the stores, then we have 6 hold-ons. And it's not exactly coherent. Yes, I think my interpretation is that the white body text in a card, I want the title's body text to be the same size as that one, and I think that's down a small notch. Then the cards themselves, they possibly, the title should be one step up, and the spacing is wrong, so the actual visual top padding is not enough. The bottom padding is actually more, and so that's obviously a problem. Now, if I go into something, let's say I go into glass, now the layout changes. So below the title is now a type, store, that is small, but it's now very awkwardly placed. Then here there is no body text. Then comes the table, and that one has equal spacing, so I think that one's quite fine. But the store, the small store there is awkwardly placed. And in general, using underline is something we don't want to do, I think. Hoovering a card for now, I think, should also change the background color a little bit on transition. Quite a long transition. So the card gives a little bit of a different tone. So that's probably a little lighter in dark mode. I think the table, as in, now the table looks better. So if I'm, for instance, I'm in functions, the table has quite a wide CES column, but for the rows mode, the names aren't that wide, but they still take a lot of space. So it's not distributed in a good way. The first paragraph in the brief seems to be larger than the ones below. And here, I honestly don't think it works. Maybe it could work if done in another way, but here it quite doesn't. I don't know if this is something that you comprehend in terms of design systems or something. I think heading-wise, the top heading on a brief is, I don't know, it's possibly the same. Maybe it's I don't know how it works, if it's the same kind of component as any page that you're on. But for reading briefs, it's a bit too small because we have so many nested ones. I get that it's the size that you're on when you're on any normal page. But yeah, for a brief, I think or maybe that's because the first paragraph is bigger. Let's keep it as it is, or we nudge, no, we nudge all titles. So this one and others a little bit, maybe 10%, but otherwise it's fine. Now, the surface of a brief, again, the title should be bigger, as I think it should be overall. Not too big, but a bit bigger. But otherwise, I think the briefs read quite well. Yeah, I think that's all you need to do for now."]},"id":"1M82J5JTSJ3S13B","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}
